window.FOM_VIGNETTE_BANK={"version":"msk-w13-monday-review-1","totalQuizzes":1,"questionsPerQuiz":70,"totalQuestions":70,"quizzes":{"1":{"title":"Week 13 Monday — MSK","subtitle":"70 mixed questions","questions":[{"id":8,"topic":"L2 · Dorsal sensory root · In-House","stem":"During dissection of a spinal nerve, a student identifies separate posterior and anterior roots that converge before dividing into peripheral branches. One root is associated with a swelling containing neuronal cell bodies. What type of neuronal information travels through this root toward the spinal cord?","options":["Preganglionic sympathetic motor commands only","Voluntary skeletal-muscle motor output","Afferent sensory information from the periphery","Postganglionic parasympathetic output","Direct motor supply to intrinsic back muscles"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Primary sensory neuron central processes enter the spinal cord through dorsal roots and have cell bodies in dorsal root ganglia.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Preganglionic sympathetic axons leave the cord through ventral roots, not the sensory dorsal root.","B":"Somatic motor axons exit via ventral roots.","C":"Primary sensory neuron central processes enter the spinal cord through dorsal roots and have cell bodies in dorsal root ganglia.","D":"Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers do not constitute the dorsal root.","E":"This efferent supply travels through ventral roots and then posterior rami."},"takeaway":"Primary sensory neuron central processes enter the spinal cord through dorsal roots and have cell bodies in dorsal root ganglia.","recognition":"Dorsal sensory root — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":2},{"id":27,"topic":"L3 · Tetrodotoxin and Na channels · Step 1","stem":"A 30-year-old man develops rapidly progressive weakness after eating an improperly prepared seafood dish. He remains alert but develops shallow respirations and profound weakness of the extremities. Serum electrolyte concentrations are normal. During electrophysiologic testing, direct application of a depolarizing current to an affected axon produces an initial change in membrane voltage, but the normal regenerative upstroke fails to occur. Resting potassium conductance remains relatively preserved. Which alteration in voltage-dependent membrane conductance best explains the failure of action-potential generation?","options":["Outward K+ current mediated by resting leak channels","Inward Na+ current through voltage-gated channels","Inward Ca2+ current in smooth muscle","Electrogenic current through the Na+/K+ pump","Outward chloride movement through ligand-gated channels"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels, preventing propagated neuronal and skeletal muscle action potentials despite near-normal gradients.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Leak K+ current helps set resting potential; it does not generate fast regenerative axonal depolarization.","B":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels, preventing propagated neuronal and skeletal muscle action potentials despite near-normal gradients.","C":"This does not explain the fast sodium-dependent upstroke in peripheral nerves.","D":"The pump does not drive the rapid regenerative upstroke.","E":"Chloride current is not the principal current driving a typical fast peripheral axonal action potential."},"takeaway":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels, preventing propagated neuronal and skeletal muscle action potentials despite near-normal gradients.","recognition":"Tetrodotoxin and Na channels — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":3,"topic":"L1 · Moment arm and torque · Step 1","stem":"During an isokinetic strength evaluation, a 26-year-old recreational athlete demonstrates normal muscle activation and no evidence of tendon injury. However, the rotational force produced around the elbow changes substantially as the joint moves through its range of motion. The examiner estimates that the muscle generates similar tensile force at two joint positions, although the orientation of its tendon relative to the forearm differs. Which change in the mechanical relationship between the muscle's line of pull and the joint axis most directly accounts for the difference in measured torque?","options":["The perpendicular distance from the joint axis to the muscle’s line of pull","The tendon’s angle of insertion measured without accounting for its perpendicular moment arm","The cross-sectional area of the muscle","The fraction of motor units recruited at each position","The passive tension generated by the opposite arm’s elbow flexors"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Torque depends on force multiplied by its perpendicular moment arm; tendon orientation changes that distance across joint angles.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Torque depends on force multiplied by its perpendicular moment arm; tendon orientation changes that distance across joint angles.","B":"Insertion angle matters through its effect on the moment arm, which is the actual determinant of torque for a given force.","C":"Area influences force-producing potential, but the stem specifies comparable muscle force.","D":"Changing recruitment may alter force, but the premise explicitly holds muscle tensile force approximately constant.","E":"Passive properties of the other arm do not determine the measured joint torque."},"takeaway":"Torque depends on force multiplied by its perpendicular moment arm; tendon orientation changes that distance across joint angles.","recognition":"Moment arm and torque — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":1},{"id":12,"topic":"L2 · Epidural versus intrathecal · Step 1","stem":"A 27-year-old woman receives lumbar epidural anesthesia during labor. Shortly after medication administration, she experiences unusually rapid bilateral motor blockade, profound hypotension, and a much more extensive sensory deficit than expected from the intended anesthetic dose. The anesthesiologist suspects inadvertent delivery of medication into the cerebrospinal fluid. During the procedure, the needle had advanced beyond the posterior ligamentous structures and entered the vertebral canal. Which meningeal boundaries must have been traversed for medication to reach the compartment responsible for this unexpectedly extensive blockade?","options":["Only the ligamentum flavum","Pia mater and spinal cord gray matter","Dura mater and arachnoid mater","Only the dura mater, leaving arachnoid intact","Posterior longitudinal ligament and annulus fibrosus"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Both membranes must be traversed to access the subarachnoid CSF compartment from the epidural region.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Passing ligamentum flavum can reach the epidural space but not the intrathecal CSF.","B":"These are not required or appropriate targets for access to CSF.","C":"Both membranes must be traversed to access the subarachnoid CSF compartment from the epidural region.","D":"An injection deep to dura but superficial to intact arachnoid is not subarachnoid.","E":"These are anterior vertebral structures unrelated to the intended posterior epidural approach."},"takeaway":"Both membranes must be traversed to access the subarachnoid CSF compartment from the epidural region.","recognition":"Epidural versus intrathecal — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":37,"topic":"L4 · Fibrosis and wound integrity · Step 1","stem":"A 65-year-old man is hospitalized following a large myocardial infarction. During the first several days, the affected myocardium undergoes progressive removal of damaged cells and extracellular material. Histologic examination later in the healing process reveals increasing numbers of activated connective-tissue cells depositing collagen within the injured region. A research intervention selectively prevents these cells from synthesizing new extracellular matrix while leaving inflammatory-cell recruitment intact. How would this intervention most directly affect the mechanical integrity of the healing ventricular wall?","options":["Reduced tensile integrity of the injured ventricular wall because a stabilizing collagen scar fails to form","Greater immediate contractility of the dead myocardial region","Increased formation of new functional cardiac muscle from dense fibrous tissue","Selective restoration of normal coronary arteries","Rapid mineralization of the necrotic region"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Activated fibroblasts normally lay down collagen that provides structural support; suppressing deposition weakens the healing wall.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Activated fibroblasts normally lay down collagen that provides structural support; suppressing deposition weakens the healing wall.","B":"Dead myocytes do not recover contraction simply because collagen synthesis is blocked.","C":"Fibrotic tissue does not differentiate into functional myocardium through inhibition of collagen.","D":"Matrix blockade does not reverse the vascular occlusion responsible for infarction.","E":"Calcification is not the direct predicted result of preventing fibroblast collagen deposition."},"takeaway":"Activated fibroblasts normally lay down collagen that provides structural support; suppressing deposition weakens the healing wall.","recognition":"Fibrosis and wound integrity — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":4},{"id":29,"topic":"L3 · Demyelination and electrical conduction · Step 1","stem":"Two isolated peripheral axons have comparable diameters and normal sodium and potassium concentration gradients. One has intact myelin, while the other has undergone segmental loss of its insulating sheath. Stimulation produces markedly slower impulse transmission through the demyelinated segment, and some action potentials fail to propagate beyond it. Which changes in membrane electrical properties caused by myelin loss best explain the reduced conduction velocity and intermittent conduction failure?","options":["Reduced membrane capacitance and increased resistance across the demyelinated internode","Compensatory redistribution of sodium channels into exposed internodal membrane","Complete elimination of the axonal potassium gradient","Increased capacitance and leak across exposed internodal membrane, reducing efficient depolarization of the next excitable region","Increased membrane resistance at the exposed internode"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Myelin insulates internodes; loss increases transmembrane current loss and electrical charge requirements, slowing or blocking saltatory propagation.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Myelin loss generally increases effective capacitance and reduces insulation; it does not improve passive spread.","B":"Sodium-channel redistribution can aid conduction after demyelination; it does not explain the immediate conduction loss from current leakage.","C":"Demyelination does not itself erase ion concentration gradients.","D":"Myelin insulates internodes; loss increases transmembrane current loss and electrical charge requirements, slowing or blocking saltatory propagation.","E":"Higher membrane resistance reduces current leak, whereas demyelination exposes a lower-resistance pathway that reduces efficient propagation."},"takeaway":"Myelin insulates internodes; loss increases transmembrane current loss and electrical charge requirements, slowing or blocking saltatory propagation.","recognition":"Demyelination and electrical conduction — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":19,"topic":"L2 · Denticulate ligament · Step 1","stem":"A 43-year-old woman undergoes resection of an intradural extramedullary tumor in the thoracic vertebral canal. During surgery, several lateral attachments between the spinal cord and surrounding dura must be divided to mobilize the cord safely. Postoperative imaging shows no residual tumor, but the surgeon notes that the normal structures restricting lateral displacement of the spinal cord have been disrupted. Which anatomical specialization was divided during the procedure?","options":["Denticulate ligaments derived from pia mater","Arachnoid trabeculae","Cauda equina roots","Filum terminale","Posterior longitudinal ligament"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The denticulate ligaments are lateral pial extensions that help suspend the spinal cord within the dural sac.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The denticulate ligaments are lateral pial extensions that help suspend the spinal cord within the dural sac.","B":"Trabeculae cross the subarachnoid space as delicate strands but are not the characteristic paired toothlike lateral anchoring structures.","C":"These are neural roots, not a pia-derived anchoring specialization.","D":"The filum is an inferior longitudinal anchoring structure, not multiple lateral toothlike attachments.","E":"This is a vertebral ligament posterior to vertebral bodies and outside the meningeal sac."},"takeaway":"The denticulate ligaments are lateral pial extensions that help suspend the spinal cord within the dural sac.","recognition":"Denticulate ligament — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":23,"topic":"L3 · Hypokalemia and excitability · Step 1","stem":"A 49-year-old woman taking a diuretic for hypertension presents with generalized weakness and intermittent muscle cramps. Her serum potassium concentration is 2.6 mEq/L. Nerve and skeletal muscle fibers retain their normal intracellular potassium concentrations, but the extracellular ionic environment has changed substantially. During electrophysiologic testing, stronger stimulation is required to produce a propagated response compared with measurements obtained before treatment. Which change in the resting membrane voltage explains the increased stimulation threshold?","options":["Depolarization toward a more positive E_K","Increased resting sodium conductance driving the membrane toward the sodium equilibrium potential","Selective increase in resting sodium permeability","Enhanced inward calcium current that shifts resting voltage toward threshold","Hyperpolarization toward a more negative potassium equilibrium potential"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Low extracellular K+ makes E_K more negative and can hyperpolarize the membrane, increasing the depolarization needed for threshold.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Decreasing extracellular K+ drives E_K more negative, not more positive.","B":"An increased resting sodium conductance would tend to depolarize the membrane; the stated isolated extracellular potassium decrease moves the potassium equilibrium voltage negative.","C":"There is no evidence that sodium channels opened at rest.","D":"An enhanced calcium conductance would favor depolarization, whereas reduced extracellular potassium primarily shifts the resting potential toward a more negative E_K.","E":"Low extracellular K+ makes E_K more negative and can hyperpolarize the membrane, increasing the depolarization needed for threshold."},"takeaway":"Low extracellular K+ makes E_K more negative and can hyperpolarize the membrane, increasing the depolarization needed for threshold.","recognition":"Hypokalemia and excitability — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":14,"topic":"L2 · Posterior spinal nerve ramus · Step 1","stem":"A 36-year-old man sustains a penetrating injury immediately lateral to the spinous processes in the upper thoracic region. Several weeks later, he has localized sensory loss over the posterior trunk and weakness of small muscles responsible for stabilizing the vertebral column. Strength and cutaneous sensation over the anterior thorax and upper limbs remain intact. Imaging excludes damage to the spinal cord or complete spinal nerve. At what point along the spinal nerve pathway is the injury most likely located?","options":["Complete thoracic spinal nerve proximal to division","Posterior ramus after division of the mixed spinal nerve","Ventral root before joining the dorsal root","Dorsal root proximal to its ganglion","Ventral ramus supplying the intercostal region"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic back muscles and skin of the posterior trunk, fitting selective posterior deficits.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A complete spinal nerve injury could also affect the ventral ramus and anterior trunk structures.","B":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic back muscles and skin of the posterior trunk, fitting selective posterior deficits.","C":"A ventral-root injury would affect motor fibers without directly explaining the focal posterior cutaneous sensory loss.","D":"A root lesion would primarily affect sensory afferents and would not isolate motor supply to deep back muscles.","E":"Ventral rami supply anterior/lateral trunk territory rather than intrinsic back musculature."},"takeaway":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic back muscles and skin of the posterior trunk, fitting selective posterior deficits.","recognition":"Posterior spinal nerve ramus — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":21,"topic":"L3 · Ion permeability changes · Intermediate","stem":"A researcher increases a resting neuron’s sodium permeability without altering ion concentrations or potassium permeability. Which immediate shift in membrane potential is expected?","options":["Hyperpolarization toward the K+ equilibrium potential","Depolarization toward the Na+ equilibrium potential","No change because only ATPase activity determines membrane voltage","Immediate loss of both sodium and potassium concentration gradients","Reversal of the electrical sign of the sodium equilibrium potential"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Increasing permeability to an ion shifts membrane voltage toward that ion’s electrochemical equilibrium potential; E_Na is more positive.","optionExplanations":{"A":"K+ permeability did not increase, and the newly enhanced Na+ conductance does not drive voltage toward E_K.","B":"Increasing permeability to an ion shifts membrane voltage toward that ion’s electrochemical equilibrium potential; E_Na is more positive.","C":"Passive channel permeability directly influences membrane potential.","D":"Changing permeability briefly does not instantaneously eliminate bulk transmembrane gradients.","E":"The equilibrium potential depends on sodium’s concentration ratio and charge, neither of which changed."},"takeaway":"Increasing permeability to an ion shifts membrane voltage toward that ion’s electrochemical equilibrium potential; E_Na is more positive.","recognition":"Ion permeability changes — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":3},{"id":5,"topic":"L1 · Collateral arterial circulation · Step 1","stem":"A 34-year-old man sustains a traumatic elbow dislocation complicated by injury to a major artery. Vascular imaging demonstrates markedly reduced flow through the injured segment, but contrast reaches the distal forearm through several smaller vessels that reconnect with the main arterial circulation beyond the obstruction. The patient has preserved capillary refill despite decreased distal pulses. If these smaller communicating vessels had also been disrupted, which physiological consequence would be most likely in the tissues distal to the injury?","options":["Increased venous return compensating for the lost arterial branches","Preservation of tissue oxygenation by a higher distal venous pressure","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure with improved nutrient delivery","Increased distal ischemia because alternative arterial flow pathways are lost","Improved distal perfusion through diversion of blood"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Anastomoses permit collateral circulation around a blockage; losing them reduces oxygenated blood reaching the distal tissues.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Venous return does not supply oxygenated blood past an obstructed arterial segment.","B":"An increased venous pressure cannot restore arterial supply after all collateral routes are lost.","C":"Higher capillary pressure would not replace lost arterial inflow and can worsen edema.","D":"Anastomoses permit collateral circulation around a blockage; losing them reduces oxygenated blood reaching the distal tissues.","E":"Destroying collaterals removes alternate routes and worsens perfusion."},"takeaway":"Anastomoses permit collateral circulation around a blockage; losing them reduces oxygenated blood reaching the distal tissues.","recognition":"Collateral arterial circulation — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":1},{"id":41,"topic":"L5 · Wallerian degeneration · Step 1","stem":"A 27-year-old man sustains a deep forearm laceration that completely transects a peripheral nerve. During surgical follow-up, a small specimen is obtained from the distal portion of the injured nerve. Microscopy reveals fragmentation of axonal profiles, irregular pale inclusions derived from damaged insulating material, and numerous macrophages clearing cellular debris. In contrast, neuronal cell bodies proximal to the injury remain intact. Which relationship between axonal continuity and the maintenance of distal nerve segments most directly explains these histologic changes?","options":["Wallerian degeneration of the disconnected distal axon segment","Primary destruction of the neuron’s nucleus with preservation of the distal axon","Regeneration of myelin around otherwise intact distal fibers without breakdown","Primary segmental Schwann-cell demyelination with preserved continuous axons","Local loss of peripheral myelin without fragmentation of the axonal cytoskeleton"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Distal axons depend on continuity with the neuronal cell body; after transection, distal axon and myelin break down and are cleared.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Distal axons depend on continuity with the neuronal cell body; after transection, distal axon and myelin break down and are cleared.","B":"The neuronal cell bodies remain alive; distal degeneration is the described event.","C":"The biopsy shows axonal fragmentation and myelin debris, not intact fibers.","D":"Segmental demyelination can disrupt conduction, but the observed distal axonal fragmentation after complete transection signifies Wallerian degeneration.","E":"Loss of myelin alone would not explain the fragmented distal axon profiles present in the specimen."},"takeaway":"Distal axons depend on continuity with the neuronal cell body; after transection, distal axon and myelin break down and are cleared.","recognition":"Wallerian degeneration — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":9,"topic":"L2 · Dermatomes versus nerve territories · Intermediate","stem":"A patient develops reduced sensation across the lateral forearm following injury to a cervical spinal nerve root. Examination reveals that the sensory abnormality does not precisely match the cutaneous territory of any single named peripheral nerve. A second patient with a lesion of a distal peripheral nerve demonstrates a more restricted sensory deficit. Which difference in the anatomical organization of spinal nerve roots and peripheral nerves explains the contrasting sensory distributions?","options":["Root lesions affect only motor axons, whereas distal nerves contain sensory fibers","Each peripheral nerve arises exclusively from a single spinal root","A spinal segment contributes to a dermatome, whereas named peripheral nerves redistribute fibers from multiple spinal levels","Dermatomes are determined by arterial territories, whereas peripheral nerves follow veins","Sensory nerves enter the spinal cord via the anterior root"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"A dermatomal pattern reflects segmental afferents; named nerves often contain mixed spinal-level contributions and have distinct cutaneous territories.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Spinal dorsal roots carry sensory afferents; root lesions can produce dermatomal sensory loss.","B":"Many peripheral nerves contain fibers from multiple spinal levels.","C":"A dermatomal pattern reflects segmental afferents; named nerves often contain mixed spinal-level contributions and have distinct cutaneous territories.","D":"Sensory distributions are neural, not vascular, territories.","E":"Sensory afferents enter through dorsal roots."},"takeaway":"A dermatomal pattern reflects segmental afferents; named nerves often contain mixed spinal-level contributions and have distinct cutaneous territories.","recognition":"Dermatomes versus nerve territories — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":2},{"id":30,"topic":"L3 · Sodium channel inactivation in depolarization block · Step 1","stem":"A 19-year-old man experiences recurrent episodes of skeletal muscle weakness associated with sustained membrane depolarization. During an episode, intracellular recordings reveal that muscle fibers initially respond to stimulation, but repetitive stimulation produces progressively smaller action potentials until the fibers become electrically unresponsive. Pharmacologic testing demonstrates persistent inward sodium current after the initial depolarization, even though extracellular sodium and potassium concentrations remain normal. Which change in the distribution of voltage-gated sodium channels among their functional states most directly explains the progressive failure to generate additional action potentials?","options":["An increased fraction is recovered to the closed but activatable state","A larger fraction of channels cycles directly from open to resting closed without inactivation","A shift toward voltage-gated calcium currents carrying the fast skeletal-muscle upstroke","An increased fraction of channels is inactivated and unavailable for another fast upstroke","The total number of channels doubles within seconds"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Persistent depolarization holds many fast Na+ channels in their inactivated conformation, impairing repeated action potentials.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Recovered closed channels are available to open with a new threshold stimulus, which would favor excitability.","B":"Normal voltage-gated sodium channels undergo inactivation after opening; persistent depolarization reduces availability rather than restoring a fully activatable pool.","C":"The fast skeletal-muscle action-potential upstroke is predominantly sodium dependent; calcium currents do not explain the progressive Na-channel unavailability.","D":"Persistent depolarization holds many fast Na+ channels in their inactivated conformation, impairing repeated action potentials.","E":"Channel expression is not the rapid mechanism specified by the recorded electrical behavior."},"takeaway":"Persistent depolarization holds many fast Na+ channels in their inactivated conformation, impairing repeated action potentials.","recognition":"Sodium channel inactivation in depolarization block — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":43,"topic":"L5 · GBS primary demyelination · Step 1","stem":"A 26-year-old woman develops progressive bilateral lower-extremity weakness approximately two weeks after a gastrointestinal illness. Examination reveals markedly reduced reflexes and distal paresthesias. Nerve conduction studies show substantial slowing of conduction velocity, prolonged distal motor latencies, and temporal dispersion of compound muscle action potentials. An alternative form of peripheral neuropathy can produce similar weakness through primary destruction of axons rather than loss of their insulating sheaths. Which histologic abnormality would most strongly support a primary demyelinating process rather than a predominantly axonal neuropathy?","options":["Widespread absence of distal axonal profiles with no surviving fibers","Degeneration of anterior horn motor-neuron nuclei in spinal gray matter","Segmental loss of Schwann-cell myelin along relatively preserved axons","Loss of peripheral myelin only after complete degeneration of every affected motor axon","Preserved Schwann-cell myelin with selective degeneration of dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"A primary demyelinating disorder injures myelin segments while many axons remain intact, producing slow, dispersed conduction.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Primary axonal loss is characterized by degeneration of axons.","B":"A central motor-neuron lesion is not the usual primary peripheral demyelinating pathology.","C":"A primary demyelinating disorder injures myelin segments while many axons remain intact, producing slow, dispersed conduction.","D":"Primary demyelination is defined by prominent myelin injury with relative axonal preservation, not obligatory complete axonal loss first.","E":"Primary demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy can affect sensory and motor fibers, but its key pathology is segmental peripheral myelin damage."},"takeaway":"A primary demyelinating disorder injures myelin segments while many axons remain intact, producing slow, dispersed conduction.","recognition":"GBS primary demyelination — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":35,"topic":"L4 · Mast cell histamine degranulation · Step 1","stem":"A 15-year-old girl with a history of severe food allergy develops generalized urticaria, hypotension, and respiratory difficulty shortly after allergen exposure. In a laboratory model using tissue from a previously sensitized individual, investigators observe rapid enlargement of gaps between vascular endothelial cells before significant accumulation of circulating inflammatory leukocytes. A second experimental preparation has normal numbers of resident connective-tissue immune cells but cannot release their stored granule contents. Which early vascular response would be most directly reduced in the second preparation?","options":["Cross-linking of fibrillar collagen in tendons","Macrophage phagocytosis of damaged tissue over subsequent days","Proliferation of fibroblasts after several days","Deposition of glycosaminoglycans in cartilage ECM","Histamine-mediated dilation of endothelial gaps and plasma extravasation"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Mast-cell granule exocytosis releases histamine, promoting acute vascular permeability and edema.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Collagen cross-linking is not the rapid granule-mediated vascular event.","B":"Later macrophage functions are separate from the immediate mast-cell histamine response.","C":"Fibroblast-mediated repair occurs later than the minutes-scale response described.","D":"Cartilage ground substance is unrelated to acute endothelial leak in allergy.","E":"Mast-cell granule exocytosis releases histamine, promoting acute vascular permeability and edema."},"takeaway":"Mast-cell granule exocytosis releases histamine, promoting acute vascular permeability and edema.","recognition":"Mast cell histamine degranulation — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":4},{"id":25,"topic":"L3 · Refractory periods · Step 1","stem":"During a nerve physiology experiment, two suprathreshold electrical stimuli are delivered to the same axon in rapid succession. The first produces a normal propagated action potential, but the second fails to generate another response. Increasing the strength of the second stimulus does not restore excitability during the earliest portion of this interval. Later, a sufficiently strong stimulus can produce a second response before the membrane has fully returned to its resting state. Which sequence of channel-state changes explains these two different periods of reduced excitability?","options":["Initial Na+ channel inactivation followed by continued elevation of K+ conductance while Na+ availability recovers","Complete absence of Na+ concentration gradients followed by instantaneous regeneration","Synaptic vesicle depletion followed by reuptake","Initial chloride-channel opening followed by receptor internalization","Permanent sodium-channel closure followed by new protein synthesis"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"During the absolute period many Na+ channels are inactivated; the relative period combines partial Na+ recovery with afterhyperpolarizing K+ conductance.","optionExplanations":{"A":"During the absolute period many Na+ channels are inactivated; the relative period combines partial Na+ recovery with afterhyperpolarizing K+ conductance.","B":"Bulk Na+ gradients do not disappear with each individual action potential.","C":"Axonal electrical refractoriness can occur in an isolated axon independent of chemical synapses.","D":"The described refractoriness is explained chiefly by voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channel kinetics.","E":"Refractory periods resolve in milliseconds without synthesis of replacement channels."},"takeaway":"During the absolute period many Na+ channels are inactivated; the relative period combines partial Na+ recovery with afterhyperpolarizing K+ conductance.","recognition":"Refractory periods — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":38,"topic":"L4 · Aggrecan GAG compression resistance · Step 1","stem":"Investigators examine articular cartilage obtained from a patient with an inherited defect affecting the synthesis of highly charged extracellular matrix polysaccharides. Microscopy shows that the major collagen framework is relatively preserved, but the cartilage has diminished water retention and undergoes excessive deformation under compressive loading. Which property of the affected extracellular matrix molecules normally allows cartilage to resist compression, and why does loss of this property impair tissue function?","options":["Alignment of skeletal muscle contractile filaments","Formation of voltage-gated sodium channels","Formation of type I collagen bundles parallel to tendon length","Osmotic water retention by negatively charged glycosaminoglycans bound to proteoglycans","Production of brown adipocyte mitochondria"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"GAG fixed negative charges attract water and counterions, helping hydrated cartilage matrix withstand compressive load.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Muscle filaments are not extracellular cartilage ground substance.","B":"Electrical channels do not explain cartilage compressive resistance.","C":"The stem concerns cartilage compressive mechanics and says the collagen network remains preserved.","D":"GAG fixed negative charges attract water and counterions, helping hydrated cartilage matrix withstand compressive load.","E":"Adipose mitochondrial content is unrelated to cartilage compression."},"takeaway":"GAG fixed negative charges attract water and counterions, helping hydrated cartilage matrix withstand compressive load.","recognition":"Aggrecan GAG compression resistance — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":4},{"id":42,"topic":"L5 · Schwann bands and nerve regeneration · Step 1","stem":"Two patients sustain peripheral nerve injuries of comparable severity. In the first, the severed nerve ends are precisely approximated during repair, and motor function gradually returns. In the second, the nerve ends remain widely separated, and regenerating axons fail to reach their original distal targets. Biopsies obtained several weeks later demonstrate viable neuronal cell bodies and evidence of axonal outgrowth in both patients. Which difference in the local cellular environment most directly accounts for the improved recovery in the first patient?","options":["Formation of a dense, disorganized collagen scar between severed nerve ends","Extensive demyelination without Schwann-cell proliferation","Aligned proliferating Schwann cells form regeneration pathways that direct axonal sprouts toward their former distal targets","Macrophage clearance of distal axonal debris without formation of guiding Schwann-cell bands","Interruption of retrograde trophic signaling to prevent synapse formation"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Schwann-cell bands of Büngner guide regenerating axons when a continuous route through the distal nerve is preserved.","optionExplanations":{"A":"An obstructing scar would impede rather than guide regenerating axons back to their targets.","B":"Demyelination without Schwann-cell guidance does not establish a regenerative track.","C":"Schwann-cell bands of Büngner guide regenerating axons when a continuous route through the distal nerve is preserved.","D":"Debris clearance contributes to repair but does not by itself supply the aligned cellular route needed for successful axonal regrowth.","E":"Loss of target-derived trophic signals tends to impair neuronal maintenance rather than restore function."},"takeaway":"Schwann-cell bands of Büngner guide regenerating axons when a continuous route through the distal nerve is preserved.","recognition":"Schwann bands and nerve regeneration — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":22,"topic":"L3 · Hyperkalemic depolarization block · Step 1","stem":"A 67-year-old man with advanced chronic kidney disease presents with weakness, palpitations, and a serum potassium concentration of 7.4 mEq/L. The ECG demonstrates peaked T waves and widening of the QRS complex. Initially, the altered extracellular potassium concentration causes a shift in resting membrane voltage that can increase excitability. However, as the disturbance becomes severe, effective propagation of action potentials diminishes despite the membrane remaining relatively depolarized. Which change in the functional availability of voltage-gated ion channels most directly accounts for this progression?","options":["Increased recovery of fast sodium channels into the closed activatable state","Increased membrane density of functional acetylcholine receptors","Reduction of the sodium electrochemical driving force without altered channel availability","Slowed deactivation of delayed-rectifier potassium channels after each action potential","Accumulation of voltage-gated sodium channels in the inactivated state"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Sustained depolarization maintains many fast Na+ channels inactivated, leaving too few available for regenerative action potentials.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Increased channel availability would support subsequent action potentials rather than explain failure during sustained depolarization.","B":"Receptor density is not the primary explanation for severe hyperkalemic inexcitability.","C":"Alterations in sodium driving force are not the key mechanism of reduced excitability during severe potassium-mediated sustained depolarization.","D":"Delayed-rectifier behavior influences repolarization, but sustained Na-channel inactivation accounts most directly for the depolarization block.","E":"Sustained depolarization maintains many fast Na+ channels inactivated, leaving too few available for regenerative action potentials."},"takeaway":"Sustained depolarization maintains many fast Na+ channels inactivated, leaving too few available for regenerative action potentials.","recognition":"Hyperkalemic depolarization block — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":6,"topic":"L1 · Lymphatic drainage · Step 1","stem":"Several months after treatment for a malignancy, a 53-year-old woman develops persistent swelling of one upper extremity following removal of regional lymph nodes. Doppler ultrasonography excludes venous thrombosis, and cardiac and renal evaluations are unremarkable. Examination reveals progressive tissue enlargement and a sensation of heaviness rather than pain associated with acute inflammation. Impairment of which normal transport process most directly explains the persistent increase in interstitial fluid and protein content?","options":["Delivery of oxygenated blood through large arteries","Return of excess interstitial fluid and escaped plasma proteins to the venous circulation","Increased venous hydrostatic pressure from a proximal venous thrombosis","Filtration of blood through glomerular capillaries","Decreased protein synthesis within the interstitial compartment"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Lymphatic capillaries normally collect tissue fluid and leaked proteins, ultimately returning lymph to venous blood.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Arterial delivery is not primarily interrupted by lymph-node removal.","B":"Lymphatic capillaries normally collect tissue fluid and leaked proteins, ultimately returning lymph to venous blood.","C":"Venous thrombosis can cause edema, but venous imaging is normal and the swelling began after lymph-node removal.","D":"Renal filtration is distinct from local lymphatic drainage.","E":"Less interstitial protein production does not account for failure to clear normal leaked plasma proteins."},"takeaway":"Lymphatic capillaries normally collect tissue fluid and leaked proteins, ultimately returning lymph to venous blood.","recognition":"Lymphatic drainage — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":1},{"id":64,"topic":"L7 · Posterolateral disc anatomy · Step 1","stem":"A 45-year-old warehouse worker experiences sudden lower-back pain while lifting a heavy object. MRI reveals protrusion of the central gelatinous material of an intervertebral disc through a defect in its surrounding fibrous ring. The protrusion is eccentric rather than directly posterior. The treating physician notes that the ligament running along the posterior surfaces of vertebral bodies provides uneven reinforcement across the width of the disc. Which anatomical relationship makes posterolateral disc displacement more likely than a strictly midline protrusion?","options":["The anterior longitudinal ligament is absent from the lumbar spine","The annulus fibrosus exists only on the anterior half of the disc","The posterior longitudinal ligament reinforces the midline posterior disc more than its lateral margins","The facet joints are cartilaginous symphyses located inside the disc","The nucleus pulposus is a rigid bony mass attached to the pedicles"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"A relatively less reinforced posterolateral disc region allows nucleus material to protrude adjacent to the posterior longitudinal ligament.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The anterior longitudinal ligament is present and primarily resists extension.","B":"The annulus surrounds the nucleus and may develop defects posterolaterally.","C":"A relatively less reinforced posterolateral disc region allows nucleus material to protrude adjacent to the posterior longitudinal ligament.","D":"Facet joints are posterior plane synovial joints, not disc components.","E":"The nucleus is gelatinous material, not bone."},"takeaway":"A relatively less reinforced posterolateral disc region allows nucleus material to protrude adjacent to the posterior longitudinal ligament.","recognition":"Posterolateral disc anatomy — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":10,"topic":"L2 · Sensory root localization · Step 1","stem":"A 46-year-old woman undergoes surgical removal of a mass extending into a lumbar intervertebral foramen. Following the procedure, she develops impaired joint-position sensation and reduced perception of light touch within a segmental distribution of the lower extremity. Strength remains normal, but a deep tendon reflex associated with the affected spinal level is diminished. Needle electromyography reveals preserved voluntary motor-unit recruitment without signs of denervation. MRI demonstrates a focal abnormality in the dorsal root/dorsal root ganglion region immediately before formation of the mixed spinal nerve. Which component of the spinal nerve pathway best accounts for the combination of sensory loss, diminished reflex activity, and preserved voluntary motor function?","options":["Ventral root motor axons","Anterior horn motor neuron cell bodies","Posterior ramus after the mixed spinal nerve divides","Preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the lateral horn","Dorsal root or dorsal root ganglion sensory fibers"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Selective injury to primary sensory afferents can diminish sensation and the afferent limb of reflexes while sparing somatic motor output.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A ventral root injury should produce a motor deficit and signs of denervation.","B":"Loss of these neurons should impair voluntary muscle contraction.","C":"A posterior ramus lesion mainly affects intrinsic back musculature and posterior trunk sensation.","D":"These regulate autonomic output rather than directly conveying limb cutaneous sensation.","E":"Selective injury to primary sensory afferents can diminish sensation and the afferent limb of reflexes while sparing somatic motor output."},"takeaway":"Selective injury to primary sensory afferents can diminish sensation and the afferent limb of reflexes while sparing somatic motor output.","recognition":"Sensory root localization — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":52,"topic":"L6 · Superior cervical sympathetic ascent · Step 1","stem":"A 44-year-old woman undergoes resection of a cervical mass extending close to the sympathetic trunk. Postoperatively, she develops ipsilateral pupillary constriction, mild ptosis, and decreased sweating over one side of the face. Strength and sensation in the upper extremities remain intact. The surgeon suspects that ascending sympathetic fibers supplying the head were interrupted before reaching their superior cervical synaptic station. Why can disruption of fibers arising from thoracic spinal cord levels produce autonomic deficits in the head despite the absence of preganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell bodies in the cervical spinal cord?","options":["Preganglionic sympathetic neurons arise from cervical lateral horns and synapse near their segment of origin","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers ascend directly within the internal carotid plexus without synapsing","Postganglionic sympathetic somata reside in the brainstem near cranial parasympathetic nuclei","Preganglionic sympathetic axons enter the spinal nerve through dorsal rather than ventral roots","Thoracic preganglionic axons can ascend in the sympathetic trunk to synapse in a superior cervical ganglion"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Ascending sympathetic chain fibers link thoracic preganglionic origins with cervical postganglionic neurons supplying the head.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons originate in thoracolumbar cord segments, and those targeting the head ascend in the chain before synapsing.","B":"The internal carotid plexus carries postganglionic fibers after they have synapsed in the superior cervical ganglion.","C":"Sympathetic neurons supplying the head have postganglionic somata in the superior cervical ganglion, not cranial parasympathetic nuclei.","D":"Sympathetic preganglionic efferents leave the cord through ventral roots and reach the sympathetic trunk via white rami.","E":"Ascending sympathetic chain fibers link thoracic preganglionic origins with cervical postganglionic neurons supplying the head."},"takeaway":"Ascending sympathetic chain fibers link thoracic preganglionic origins with cervical postganglionic neurons supplying the head.","recognition":"Superior cervical sympathetic ascent — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":63,"topic":"L7 · Lumbar stenosis cauda equina compression · Step 1","stem":"A 72-year-old man reports bilateral lower-extremity discomfort and weakness after walking short distances. The symptoms improve with sitting and are sometimes relieved by leaning forward. Examination demonstrates intact distal arterial pulses. Lumbar MRI shows degenerative disc bulging and osteophyte formation that narrow the vertebral canal at several levels. Which anatomical structures are most likely being compressed by these degenerative changes, accounting for the patient's bilateral lower-extremity symptoms?","options":["Lumbar dorsal root ganglia located within the intervertebral foramina","Lumbar sympathetic trunks running along the anterolateral vertebral bodies","Femoral nerve trunks distal to the inguinal ligament","Exiting lumbar roots confined to narrowed intervertebral foramina","Descending lumbar and sacral nerve roots within the cauda equina"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Lumbar central stenosis can constrict cauda equina nerve roots, producing bilateral neurogenic claudication.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Ganglia in the foramina can be affected by foraminal narrowing, but central lumbar canal stenosis primarily impinges the descending cauda equina roots.","B":"The sympathetic trunks are outside the central vertebral canal and would not explain the bilateral neurogenic claudication associated with central stenosis.","C":"A distal femoral neuropathy would not account for multilevel root compression within the stenotic lumbar vertebral canal.","D":"Foraminal stenosis can affect exiting roots; the described central canal narrowing primarily compromises the descending nerve roots of the cauda equina.","E":"Lumbar central stenosis can constrict cauda equina nerve roots, producing bilateral neurogenic claudication."},"takeaway":"Lumbar central stenosis can constrict cauda equina nerve roots, producing bilateral neurogenic claudication.","recognition":"Lumbar stenosis cauda equina compression — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":39,"topic":"L5 · Perineurium · In-House","stem":"A peripheral nerve cross-section shows several fascicles surrounded by a specialized connective-tissue sheath. Which layer encloses one fascicle rather than a single nerve fiber or the entire nerve?","options":["Endoneurium","Epineurium","Endoneurial connective tissue surrounding myelinated axons","Outer epineurial connective tissue between fascicles","Perineurium"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Perineurium forms the sheath around each peripheral nerve fascicle and contributes to the blood-nerve barrier.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Endoneurium surrounds individual fibers within a fascicle.","B":"Epineurium surrounds the entire nerve and inter-fascicular tissue.","C":"Endoneurium encloses individual nerve fibers and not the fascicle as a whole.","D":"Epineurium invests the overall nerve; the discrete fascicular sheath is perineurium.","E":"Perineurium forms the sheath around each peripheral nerve fascicle and contributes to the blood-nerve barrier."},"takeaway":"Perineurium forms the sheath around each peripheral nerve fascicle and contributes to the blood-nerve barrier.","recognition":"Perineurium — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":5},{"id":61,"topic":"L7 · Pars fracture and spondylolisthesis · Step 1","stem":"A 19-year-old gymnast presents with gradually progressive lower-back pain that worsens during backbends and prolonged standing. The pain has developed over several months without a single traumatic event. Neurologic examination is normal, and straight-leg raising does not reproduce the symptoms. CT demonstrates a small stress fracture involving the bony segment connecting the superior and inferior articular processes of L5. If repetitive loading continues without adequate healing, which additional abnormality of vertebral alignment may develop as a consequence of this defect?","options":["Anterior translation of L4 relative to L5 caused by degenerative facet arthropathy","Anterior translation of L5 relative to S1 (isthmic spondylolisthesis)","Posterior translation of L5 relative to S1 without anterior slippage","Progressive L5 vertebral-body compression with local kyphotic angulation","Isolated L5–S1 disc-space narrowing without a change in vertebral alignment"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"A pars interarticularis defect can permit anterior vertebral slip, particularly at the lumbosacral junction.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Degenerative L4–L5 slippage is a distinct mechanism; the documented pars defect at L5 predisposes to L5 movement relative to S1.","B":"A pars interarticularis defect can permit anterior vertebral slip, particularly at the lumbosacral junction.","C":"An L5 pars defect is associated with isthmic anterior spondylolisthesis, not predominantly posterior retrolisthesis.","D":"A pars stress fracture lies in the posterior elements and does not itself indicate anterior vertebral-body compression.","E":"Disc-space degeneration can coexist, but the question asks for the characteristic alignment complication of a pars interarticularis defect."},"takeaway":"A pars interarticularis defect can permit anterior vertebral slip, particularly at the lumbosacral junction.","recognition":"Pars fracture and spondylolisthesis — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":40,"topic":"L5 · Anterograde axonal transport · Intermediate","stem":"A drug blocks microtubule-based movement of newly packaged synaptic vesicles from a neuronal soma to distant axon terminals. Which transport process is primarily impaired?","options":["Anterograde axonal transport toward the terminal","Retrograde transport toward the soma","Passive diffusion of vesicles across the plasma membrane","Saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier","Transmission across an electrical gap junction"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Anterograde microtubule-dependent movement carries secretory components from cell bodies toward distal synapses.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Anterograde microtubule-dependent movement carries secretory components from cell bodies toward distal synapses.","B":"Retrograde transport returns material and signals from axon terminals toward the cell body.","C":"Long-distance axonal vesicle movement is directed along cytoskeletal tracks.","D":"Saltatory conduction is electrical propagation along myelinated axons.","E":"Gap-junction current flow is not axonal cargo delivery."},"takeaway":"Anterograde microtubule-dependent movement carries secretory components from cell bodies toward distal synapses.","recognition":"Anterograde axonal transport — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":5},{"id":69,"topic":"L7 · Posterior rami deep intrinsic back · Step 1","stem":"A 48-year-old construction worker sustains a penetrating injury involving the deep lumbar back muscles immediately adjacent to the vertebral column. After recovery, he demonstrates marked weakness during resisted trunk extension and difficulty stabilizing the spine, while arm adduction, scapular retraction, and shoulder shrugging are preserved. MRI confirms that the injury is restricted to the intrinsic muscle compartment rather than the superficial extrinsic back muscles. Which anatomical distinction in spinal nerve branching explains the selective motor deficits?","options":["Deep intrinsic back muscles are supplied by ventral rami; superficial back muscles by posterior rami","The entire deep and superficial back musculature receives motor supply exclusively from the spinal accessory nerve","Posterior spinal nerve rami carry only cutaneous sensation and do not innervate skeletal muscles","Intrinsic back muscles are supplied by posterior spinal nerve rami, while the superficial extrinsic muscles have different motor nerve supplies","Deep back muscle contraction depends on sensory dorsal roots without any somatic motor fibers"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic paraspinal muscles; trapezius, latissimus, and rhomboids have distinct extrinsic motor supplies.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The intrinsic deep back muscles are typically supplied by posterior rami.","B":"The spinal accessory nerve supplies trapezius, while deep intrinsic back muscles are innervated segmentally by posterior rami.","C":"Posterior rami have motor branches to deep intrinsic back muscles in addition to cutaneous sensory branches.","D":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic paraspinal muscles; trapezius, latissimus, and rhomboids have distinct extrinsic motor supplies.","E":"Muscle contraction requires somatic motor axons, which reach the deep intrinsic muscles through the posterior rami."},"takeaway":"Posterior rami supply intrinsic paraspinal muscles; trapezius, latissimus, and rhomboids have distinct extrinsic motor supplies.","recognition":"Posterior rami deep intrinsic back — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":18,"topic":"L2 · Anterior spinal artery syndrome · Step 1","stem":"A 73-year-old man undergoes emergent repair of a thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm. Postoperatively, he develops bilateral weakness below a thoracic spinal cord level and reduced pain and temperature sensation. Joint position and vibration sensation are relatively preserved. MRI reveals ischemic injury involving the anterior portion of the spinal cord. Which aspect of the cord's vascular organization explains why the posterior column modalities can remain preserved despite extensive motor impairment?","options":["Selective compromise of posterior spinal artery territories","Reduced perfusion of the anterior spinal artery territory with relative sparing of posterior cord supply","Selective interruption of dorsal root ganglion blood flow","Simultaneous loss of anterior and posterior spinal arterial perfusion","Injury limited to spinal meninges"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Anterior spinal ischemia can impair descending motor tracts and anterolateral pain-temperature pathways while sparing dorsal columns.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Posterior cord ischemia would preferentially jeopardize dorsal column modalities.","B":"Anterior spinal ischemia can impair descending motor tracts and anterolateral pain-temperature pathways while sparing dorsal columns.","C":"A peripheral ganglion injury does not explain a bilateral anterior spinal cord syndrome.","D":"Global ischemia affecting anterior and posterior territories would be unlikely to preserve dorsal-column modalities.","E":"Meningeal injury alone does not explain the specific long-tract deficit pattern."},"takeaway":"Anterior spinal ischemia can impair descending motor tracts and anterolateral pain-temperature pathways while sparing dorsal columns.","recognition":"Anterior spinal artery syndrome — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":24,"topic":"L3 · Action potential sodium upstroke · Step 1","stem":"In an isolated motor neuron preparation, investigators use a voltage-sensitive recording system to measure membrane electrical activity. A control stimulus produces a rapid depolarizing upstroke followed by an overshoot and repolarization. After exposure to a selective ion-channel inhibitor, the same stimulus produces only a small local depolarization, even though resting potassium permeability remains intact. Which alteration in membrane conductance during the attempted action potential most directly explains the failure of the rapid upstroke?","options":["Large increase in delayed outward potassium current before threshold","Selective reduction in resting chloride driving force","An abrupt failure of sodium-potassium ATPase activity during the rising phase","Failure to recruit a rapidly activating inward Na+ conductance","Selective loss of internodal myelin with maintained nodal inward sodium conductance"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels normally amplifies depolarization and generates the fast upstroke and overshoot.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A substantial outward current could oppose depolarization, but it does not directly identify the normally required regenerative inward conductance.","B":"Resting chloride change is not the principal current underlying rapid neuronal upstroke.","C":"The pump maintains ion gradients over longer timescales; the fast action-potential upstroke depends immediately on voltage-gated sodium conductance.","D":"Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels normally amplifies depolarization and generates the fast upstroke and overshoot.","E":"Myelin disruption can impair propagation, but the question specifically tests failure of the rapid inward sodium current that produces the local action-potential upstroke."},"takeaway":"Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels normally amplifies depolarization and generates the fast upstroke and overshoot.","recognition":"Action potential sodium upstroke — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":66,"topic":"L7 · Latissimus thoracodorsal nerve · Step 1","stem":"A 39-year-old rock climber sustains a deep injury near the posterior axillary wall. After the injury, he struggles to pull his trunk upward toward an overhead handhold, even though his grip strength and elbow flexion remain intact. Examination reveals reduced power during shoulder extension, adduction, and medial rotation, with preserved scapular retraction and shoulder elevation. MRI shows injury involving a broad muscle extending from the lower thoracic and lumbar back to the proximal humerus. Which nerve lesion would best account for the selective movement deficits without causing weakness of the superficial scapular stabilizers?","options":["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Axillary nerve"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Thoracodorsal nerve innervates latissimus dorsi, which extends, adducts, and internally rotates the humerus, important in pulling and climbing.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Dorsal scapular injury primarily compromises rhomboids and levator scapulae, with scapular retraction deficits.","B":"This supplies trapezius and is more associated with shoulder shrugging and scapular positioning.","C":"This supplies serratus anterior and its lesion commonly produces winging, not isolated latissimus-pattern weakness.","D":"Thoracodorsal nerve innervates latissimus dorsi, which extends, adducts, and internally rotates the humerus, important in pulling and climbing.","E":"This supplies deltoid and teres minor; deficits emphasize abduction and lateral rotation, not the combination described."},"takeaway":"Thoracodorsal nerve innervates latissimus dorsi, which extends, adducts, and internally rotates the humerus, important in pulling and climbing.","recognition":"Latissimus thoracodorsal nerve — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":13,"topic":"L2 · Herpes zoster segmental sensory ganglion · Step 1","stem":"A 74-year-old man develops burning pain along the left lower thorax followed several days later by clusters of vesicles confined to the same region. The pain persists after the lesions begin to resolve. On neurologic examination, voluntary contraction of the intercostal muscles is preserved, but light touch over the affected band produces severe discomfort. The treating physician suspects reactivation of a virus that had remained dormant within primary sensory neurons. Which anatomical feature of these neurons explains how reactivation can produce a localized cutaneous eruption and sensory symptoms without directly interrupting somatic motor output?","options":["Somatic motor cell bodies in the anterior horn supply only cutaneous sensation","Ventral spinal roots house the cell bodies of somatic sensory neurons","Preganglionic sympathetic neurons directly innervate the epidermis as the main pain pathway","Primary sensory neuronal somata in a dorsal root ganglion can be affected without destroying ventral-root motor axons","Motor end plates of thoracic skeletal muscles are the normal viral latency sites"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Segmentally distributed sensory afferents account for dermatomal pain and rash while separated motor pathways remain functional.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Anterior horn neurons are motor, not primary cutaneous sensory cells.","B":"Primary sensory somata lie in dorsal root ganglia.","C":"Sensory pain fibers are not equivalent to sympathetic preganglionic motor fibers.","D":"Segmentally distributed sensory afferents account for dermatomal pain and rash while separated motor pathways remain functional.","E":"This clinical pattern localizes to sensory neurons rather than neuromuscular junctions."},"takeaway":"Segmentally distributed sensory afferents account for dermatomal pain and rash while separated motor pathways remain functional.","recognition":"Herpes zoster segmental sensory ganglion — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":49,"topic":"L5 · Retrograde dynein transport · Step 1","stem":"An experimental neuron is exposed to a drug that selectively inhibits a class of microtubule-associated motor proteins. Secretory vesicle delivery to distal axon terminals remains intact, and the neuron continues to release neurotransmitters normally during the early phase of treatment. However, after growth factors are applied exclusively to the peripheral axon terminals, the expected changes in nuclear gene expression fail to occur. Which microtubule-dependent intracellular transport mechanism has been selectively disrupted?","options":["Fast sodium current along the nodal axolemma","Retrograde dynein-dependent movement toward the cell body","Anterograde kinesin-dependent delivery of secretory vesicles","Passive movement of growth factors through extracellular CSF","Acetylcholine diffusion across the motor end plate"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Dynein drives axonal cargo and signaling complexes toward the soma, enabling distal trophic signals to influence nuclear activity.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Electrical conduction cannot carry transported growth-factor receptor complexes to the soma.","B":"Dynein drives axonal cargo and signaling complexes toward the soma, enabling distal trophic signals to influence nuclear activity.","C":"Anterograde delivery is described as preserved.","D":"The failure occurs within the neuron’s terminal-to-soma intracellular signaling pathway.","E":"Synaptic acetylcholine diffusion does not substitute for long-range trophic axonal transport."},"takeaway":"Dynein drives axonal cargo and signaling complexes toward the soma, enabling distal trophic signals to influence nuclear activity.","recognition":"Retrograde dynein transport — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":56,"topic":"L6 · Referred visceral pain convergence · Step 1","stem":"A 60-year-old man presents with episodic substernal pressure and discomfort extending into the left shoulder and medial upper arm. The symptoms occur during exertion and resolve with rest. Shoulder examination is normal, and cardiac evaluation demonstrates reversible myocardial ischemia. Despite the absence of injury to upper-extremity structures, the patient consistently perceives pain in the arm. Which arrangement of visceral and somatic sensory inputs within the spinal nervous system best explains this distribution of pain?","options":["Cardiac muscle transmits nociception by somatic motor axons of the brachial plexus","The heart is physically continuous with shoulder connective tissue","Visceral and somatic afferents converge on overlapping spinal processing circuits, causing mislocalization of visceral input","Parasympathetic outflow from the sacrum enters cervical dermatomes directly","Venous valves in the arm conduct nociceptive impulses to the heart"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Central convergence can cause higher centers to interpret cardiac nociception as arising in somatic regions with related spinal levels.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Motor axons are efferent and are not the usual visceral nociceptive route.","B":"The pain is referred neurologically, not spread by direct tissue continuity.","C":"Central convergence can cause higher centers to interpret cardiac nociception as arising in somatic regions with related spinal levels.","D":"Sacral parasympathetic pathways do not explain cardiac referred pain to upper thorax/arm.","E":"Veins do not serve as neural conduction pathways."},"takeaway":"Central convergence can cause higher centers to interpret cardiac nociception as arising in somatic regions with related spinal levels.","recognition":"Referred visceral pain convergence — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":65,"topic":"L7 · CN XI versus dorsal scapular pattern · Step 1","stem":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes excision of a mass from the posterior triangle of the neck. Postoperatively, her right shoulder appears depressed, and she has difficulty elevating the scapula against resistance. During attempted arm elevation, scapular positioning is abnormal, although distal upper-extremity strength and sensation remain intact. The surgeon suspects damage to a motor nerve that supplies a large superficial back muscle but does not provide general cutaneous sensation to the upper extremity. Which affected muscle action would most specifically help distinguish this injury from damage to the nerves supplying the rhomboids and levator scapulae?","options":["Weakness of scapular retraction with preserved shoulder shrug and superior rotation","Weakness of scapular elevation despite preservation of levator scapulae and rhomboid function","Prominent medial scapular winging during wall push-up with relatively preserved shrug","Weakness initiating glenohumeral abduction through the first 15 degrees","Reduced shoulder extension and adduction with preserved scapular elevation"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The spinal accessory nerve supplies trapezius; a lesion causes weakness of trapezius actions while dorsal scapular-innervated muscles may remain intact.","optionExplanations":{"A":"This pattern points toward rhomboid dysfunction and dorsal scapular nerve damage rather than the trapezius weakness expected after spinal accessory injury.","B":"The spinal accessory nerve supplies trapezius; a lesion causes weakness of trapezius actions while dorsal scapular-innervated muscles may remain intact.","C":"A serratus anterior or long thoracic nerve lesion produces this pattern rather than isolated trapezius weakness.","D":"Supraspinatus initiates abduction and is supplied by the suprascapular nerve; this would not distinguish accessory nerve injury.","E":"This pattern suggests latissimus dorsi or thoracodorsal nerve dysfunction rather than injury to trapezius."},"takeaway":"The spinal accessory nerve supplies trapezius; a lesion causes weakness of trapezius actions while dorsal scapular-innervated muscles may remain intact.","recognition":"CN XI versus dorsal scapular pattern — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":57,"topic":"L6 · Pelvic pain line S2–S4 · Step 1","stem":"A 47-year-old woman is evaluated for deep, poorly localized pain associated with a disorder involving an organ below the pelvic pain line. Cutaneous sensation over the pelvis is normal, and the pain is not reproducible by palpation of the abdominal wall. The treating physician explains that conscious visceral pain fibers do not always follow the same routes as sensory fibers from skin and skeletal muscle. Through which spinal cord levels and autonomic pathway would nociceptive information from this organ most likely travel?","options":["S2–S4 afferent pathways accompanying pelvic parasympathetic routes","T1–L2 sensory pathways accompanying thoracic sympathetics exclusively","L1–L2 afferents traveling in the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves","S2–S4 somatic sensory afferents carried exclusively by the pudendal nerve","Sacral ventral horn somatic efferents carried in the pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Visceral pain from below the pelvic pain line generally follows parasympathetic pathways toward S2–S4 spinal segments.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Visceral pain from below the pelvic pain line generally follows parasympathetic pathways toward S2–S4 spinal segments.","B":"Pain above the pelvic pain line typically parallels sympathetics to thoracolumbar levels.","C":"These peripheral nerves transmit predominantly somatic sensory information from specified regions and are not the visceral nociceptive pathway below the pelvic pain line.","D":"The pudendal nerve provides somatic perineal sensation, whereas the specified visceral pain generally accompanies pelvic splanchnic pathways.","E":"The requested pathway is visceral afferent signaling toward the spinal cord, not somatic motor efferent output."},"takeaway":"Visceral pain from below the pelvic pain line generally follows parasympathetic pathways toward S2–S4 spinal segments.","recognition":"Pelvic pain line S2–S4 — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":44,"topic":"L5 · CMT demyelinating Schwann pathology · Step 1","stem":"A 17-year-old boy presents with gradually progressive distal leg weakness, difficulty running, and recurrent ankle instability. Examination reveals pes cavus, distal muscular atrophy, diminished vibration sensation, and reduced ankle reflexes. His father has similar symptoms. Nerve conduction studies reveal uniformly and substantially reduced motor conduction velocities across multiple peripheral nerves, without a single focal site of conduction failure. The neurologist suspects a hereditary disorder of peripheral myelin formation. Which cellular abnormality most directly explains the diffuse slowing of conduction in this patient?","options":["Failure of ependymal cells to regulate central canal CSF","Abnormal Schwann-cell production or maintenance of peripheral myelin","Loss of astrocyte endfeet around cerebral capillaries","Destruction of anterior horn motor neurons only","Selective enlargement of satellite cells in dorsal root ganglia"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Schwann cells build PNS myelin; widespread internodal abnormalities can cause uniform slowing in hereditary demyelinating neuropathy.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Ependymal cells are CNS lining cells and are not responsible for peripheral myelination.","B":"Schwann cells build PNS myelin; widespread internodal abnormalities can cause uniform slowing in hereditary demyelinating neuropathy.","C":"Astrocytes support the CNS blood-brain barrier rather than peripheral internodal myelin.","D":"An anterior horn lesion would not explain the diffuse hereditary sensory and myelin conduction findings.","E":"Satellite cells support ganglion cell bodies rather than form the peripheral axonal myelin responsible for conduction speed."},"takeaway":"Schwann cells build PNS myelin; widespread internodal abnormalities can cause uniform slowing in hereditary demyelinating neuropathy.","recognition":"CMT demyelinating Schwann pathology — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":62,"topic":"L7 · Degenerative spondylolisthesis · Step 1","stem":"A 66-year-old woman presents with progressively worsening lower-back pain and intermittent pain radiating toward the buttocks. Lateral lumbar radiographs reveal anterior displacement of L5 relative to S1, accompanied by disc-space narrowing and degenerative changes involving the posterior vertebral joints. There is no evidence of an acute fracture through the pars interarticularis. The radiologist concludes that the displacement developed gradually because structures that ordinarily stabilize the motion segment no longer adequately resist translation. Which degenerative anatomical changes most directly explain the observed loss of vertebral stability?","options":["Isolated rupture of the ligamentum nuchae","Congenital absence of the C2 dens","Acute avulsion of the supraspinous ligament at C7","Degenerative loss of disc and facet-joint restraint causing translational instability","Increased physiologic resistance of posterior joints to translation"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Disc height loss and degenerative zygapophysial changes can impair motion-segment stabilization and contribute to degenerative spondylolisthesis.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The nuchal ligament is cervical and does not explain lower-lumbar translation.","B":"The dens is upper cervical and unrelated to L5–S1 translation.","C":"An isolated cervical ligament injury does not account for chronic lower lumbar degeneration.","D":"Disc height loss and degenerative zygapophysial changes can impair motion-segment stabilization and contribute to degenerative spondylolisthesis.","E":"Greater stabilization would oppose rather than permit abnormal vertebral slip."},"takeaway":"Disc height loss and degenerative zygapophysial changes can impair motion-segment stabilization and contribute to degenerative spondylolisthesis.","recognition":"Degenerative spondylolisthesis — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":34,"topic":"L4 · Alport and type IV collagen · Step 1","stem":"A 15-year-old boy is evaluated for persistent hematuria and progressive hearing loss. His mother reports that several male relatives developed renal disease in early adulthood. Urinalysis confirms erythrocytes and increased protein excretion, and renal evaluation reveals abnormalities of the glomerular basement membrane. A hereditary defect in a specialized extracellular matrix protein is suspected. Which molecular assembly abnormality would most directly compromise the basement membrane's structural meshwork and produce the patient's multisystem findings?","options":["Loss of type I collagen bundles in dense regular tendons","Defective type II collagen fibrils of articular cartilage","Absence of the type III collagen scaffold of reticular connective tissue","Excess elastin in the glomerular filtration membrane","Disruption of the type IV collagen network in specialized basement membranes"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Alport syndrome involves type IV collagen abnormalities affecting glomerular basement membranes and can involve cochlear and ocular structures.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Type I fibrillar collagen is important for tendons, not the disease-defining basement membrane network in Alport syndrome.","B":"Cartilage type II collagen is not the main target in the described inherited nephritis.","C":"Reticular type III collagen is not the primary defective basement membrane collagen here.","D":"Elastin is not the principal network collagen causing hereditary Alport findings.","E":"Alport syndrome involves type IV collagen abnormalities affecting glomerular basement membranes and can involve cochlear and ocular structures."},"takeaway":"Alport syndrome involves type IV collagen abnormalities affecting glomerular basement membranes and can involve cochlear and ocular structures.","recognition":"Alport and type IV collagen — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":4},{"id":55,"topic":"L6 · Myenteric motility control · Step 1","stem":"A 71-year-old woman presents with chronic abdominal distention, constipation, and episodes of intestinal pseudo-obstruction. Imaging excludes a mechanical blockage, and laboratory testing reveals no significant electrolyte disturbance. Manometry demonstrates poorly coordinated intestinal contractions despite preserved smooth-muscle tissue. Histologic examination of the affected bowel wall shows loss of enteric neurons located between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, while neurons closer to the mucosa are relatively preserved. Which intestinal function would be most directly impaired by this selective neuronal loss?","options":["Local coordination of smooth-muscle contraction and propulsive motility","Sensory transduction by dorsal root ganglia of the lower limb","Secretion mediated predominantly by neurons in the submucosal plexus","Voluntary control of skeletal muscle in the abdominal wall","Synthesis of systemic erythropoietin by the intestinal mucosa"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The myenteric plexus provides enteric motor coordination supporting peristalsis.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The myenteric plexus provides enteric motor coordination supporting peristalsis.","B":"The described damaged neurons are intrinsic to the intestinal muscular wall.","C":"Submucosal functions are comparatively preserved in the vignette.","D":"The enteric plexus regulates visceral smooth muscle rather than voluntary abdominal-wall muscle.","E":"This is not the function of the enteric muscular neural network."},"takeaway":"The myenteric plexus provides enteric motor coordination supporting peristalsis.","recognition":"Myenteric motility control — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":20,"topic":"L3 · Resting potential potassium permeability · In-House","stem":"At rest, the membrane of a spinal motor neuron is substantially more permeable to K+ than to Na+. Which consequence follows for the resting membrane potential?","options":["It equals the sodium equilibrium potential regardless of K+ gradient","It is independent of the concentration gradients for both ions","It must be exactly 0 mV because Na+ and K+ have opposite movements","It lies much closer to the K+ equilibrium potential than to the Na+ equilibrium potential","It is determined solely by active neurotransmitter secretion"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"At rest K+ conductance dominates, pulling voltage toward E_K while lesser Na+ permeability shifts it away from exact E_K.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Low relative sodium permeability prevents sodium from dominating resting potential.","B":"Ion concentration gradients and selective permeability establish membrane potential.","C":"The ionic gradients and permeability are not balanced to yield zero voltage.","D":"At rest K+ conductance dominates, pulling voltage toward E_K while lesser Na+ permeability shifts it away from exact E_K.","E":"Resting ion gradients and membrane conductance, not neurotransmitter secretion alone, determine voltage."},"takeaway":"At rest K+ conductance dominates, pulling voltage toward E_K while lesser Na+ permeability shifts it away from exact E_K.","recognition":"Resting potential potassium permeability — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":3},{"id":48,"topic":"L5 · Myenteric enteric ganglia · Step 1","stem":"A 64-year-old man undergoes intestinal resection for a localized bowel lesion. Subsequent histologic examination reveals an area of neural injury within the muscular wall of the intestine. The injured neurons have multiple processes and are distributed in small groups between smooth-muscle layers. Postoperative motility testing demonstrates impaired coordination of intestinal contractions despite preserved smooth-muscle architecture and mucosal secretory function. Which component of the peripheral nervous system most directly accounts for this selective functional deficit?","options":["Dorsal root sensory ganglion","Submucosal Meissner plexus","Myenteric Auerbach plexus","Anterior horn motor nucleus","Sympathetic chain ganglion"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"This enteric plexus lies between muscularis externa layers and coordinates gut motility, explaining selective peristaltic dysfunction.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia lie near intervertebral foramina and contain primary sensory neuron somata rather than local bowel motor circuits.","B":"The submucosal plexus more strongly influences secretion and mucosal blood flow; the lesion is between muscular layers.","C":"This enteric plexus lies between muscularis externa layers and coordinates gut motility, explaining selective peristaltic dysfunction.","D":"Somatic skeletal-muscle motor neurons in the spinal cord do not constitute the local bowel plexus.","E":"Paravertebral ganglia lie alongside the vertebral column, not between the bowel muscle layers."},"takeaway":"This enteric plexus lies between muscularis externa layers and coordinates gut motility, explaining selective peristaltic dysfunction.","recognition":"Myenteric enteric ganglia — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":53,"topic":"L6 · Prevertebral visceral sympathetic pathway · Step 1","stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes resection of a retroperitoneal tumor near the origin of the superior mesenteric artery. Following surgery, he develops abnormalities of sympathetic regulation affecting abdominal viscera, while sympathetic control of sweating and cutaneous blood vessels remains intact. The operative report notes injury to fibers traveling from the sympathetic trunk toward prevertebral ganglia. Which difference in the location of autonomic synapses explains why the visceral sympathetic pathway is disrupted while the sympathetic pathway to the body wall is preserved?","options":["Cutaneous sympathetic fibers synapse in prevertebral ganglia before returning to spinal nerves","Visceral sympathetic axons synapse in dorsal root ganglia, unlike cutaneous axons","Many visceral preganglionic axons bypass chain synapses and synapse in prevertebral ganglia, whereas cutaneous pathways synapse in paravertebral ganglia and return via gray rami","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to abdominal viscera synapse directly in dorsal root ganglia","Visceral sympathetic fibers must synapse in the nearest paravertebral ganglion before entering splanchnic nerves"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The two sympathetic routes differ in the location of synapse and target distribution, allowing selective injury.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Cutaneous pathways generally synapse in paravertebral chain ganglia and reach spinal nerves via gray rami, unlike many abdominal visceral pathways.","B":"Dorsal root ganglia contain sensory neuron cell bodies rather than sympathetic motor synapses.","C":"The two sympathetic routes differ in the location of synapse and target distribution, allowing selective injury.","D":"Dorsal root ganglia contain sensory neuron bodies; prevertebral autonomic ganglia are the sites of synapse for many abdominal sympathetic preganglionic axons.","E":"Many abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves convey preganglionic fibers through the sympathetic trunk without a chain synapse, to prevertebral ganglia."},"takeaway":"The two sympathetic routes differ in the location of synapse and target distribution, allowing selective injury.","recognition":"Prevertebral visceral sympathetic pathway — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":46,"topic":"L5 · Oligodendrocyte versus Schwann myelination · Step 1","stem":"A 32-year-old woman presents with episodic limb weakness and visual disturbances separated by several months. MRI demonstrates multiple central nervous system white matter abnormalities. Tissue examination from an active lesion reveals loss of axonal insulation and inflammatory changes, with many axons remaining structurally intact. Investigators compare the injury with a peripheral demyelinating neuropathy. Which difference in the normal myelinating cell–axon relationship would account for distinct patterns of myelin injury and repair in the two nervous system compartments?","options":["Each oligodendrocyte must myelinate only one full axon, whereas each Schwann cell myelinates several complete axons","Schwann cells form compact myelin around several separate CNS axons","Astrocytes form compact myelin around several central nervous system axons","Satellite cells myelinate segments of peripheral motor axons","One oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments of several CNS axons, whereas a peripheral Schwann cell myelinates one internode of one axon"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"CNS oligodendrocytes can extend processes to multiple axons; Schwann cells form a single myelin internode around one PNS axon.","optionExplanations":{"A":"This reverses the usual relationship between the myelinating cells and axons.","B":"Schwann cells are peripheral glia; oligodendrocytes create CNS myelin and can myelinate portions of multiple axons.","C":"Astrocytes provide homeostatic and structural support rather than producing compact CNS myelin.","D":"Satellite glia support ganglion cell bodies; Schwann cells form compact myelin around peripheral axons.","E":"CNS oligodendrocytes can extend processes to multiple axons; Schwann cells form a single myelin internode around one PNS axon."},"takeaway":"CNS oligodendrocytes can extend processes to multiple axons; Schwann cells form a single myelin internode around one PNS axon.","recognition":"Oligodendrocyte versus Schwann myelination — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":36,"topic":"L4 · Tendinopathy matrix remodeling · Step 1","stem":"A 48-year-old recreational runner presents with persistent Achilles tendon pain despite several months of reduced activity. The tendon is thickened, and ultrasound reveals irregular fiber architecture and increased vascularity. A biopsy demonstrates disorganized collagen deposition, greater cellularity than expected, and a shift toward collagen associated with tissue repair rather than normal tendon architecture. Which alteration in connective-tissue remodeling most directly accounts for the tendon's reduced mechanical efficiency and persistent susceptibility to repetitive injury?","options":["Replacement of type I collagen by mature mineralized bone matrix throughout the tendon","Replacement of organized dense regular type I collagen bundles by disorganized repair-associated matrix including relatively more type III collagen","Conversion of tendon into avascular hyaline cartilage with predominantly type II collagen","Restoration of uniformly parallel fibers and reduced cellularity","Loss of tenocyte activity with cessation of all extracellular-matrix turnover"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Tendon healing and tendinopathy can involve disordered collagen orientation and a shift toward repair-associated type III collagen, weakening efficient longitudinal load transfer.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Tendinopathy commonly involves altered tendon collagen organization and remodeling, not uniform ossification of the tendon into bone.","B":"Tendon healing and tendinopathy can involve disordered collagen orientation and a shift toward repair-associated type III collagen, weakening efficient longitudinal load transfer.","C":"The stem instead documents increased vascularity and disorganized tendinous collagen.","D":"That would resemble normal dense regular tendon more closely, not chronic pathology.","E":"Tendinopathic tissue can exhibit increased or dysregulated matrix turnover, not complete absence of cellular remodeling."},"takeaway":"Tendon healing and tendinopathy can involve disordered collagen orientation and a shift toward repair-associated type III collagen, weakening efficient longitudinal load transfer.","recognition":"Tendinopathy matrix remodeling — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":4},{"id":58,"topic":"L6 · Gray rami and sympathetic chain descent · Step 1","stem":"A 43-year-old man sustains a penetrating injury that disrupts a communicating branch between the sympathetic trunk and an L4 spinal nerve. Subsequent examination reveals impaired sweating and cutaneous vasoconstriction in part of the lower extremity, with preservation of voluntary motor function and general cutaneous sensation. Histologic examination of the damaged branch reveals predominantly unmyelinated autonomic fibers. Because preganglionic sympathetic neurons arise only from thoracic and upper lumbar spinal cord segments, which arrangement of the sympathetic chain permits these fibers to reach lower lumbar spinal nerves?","options":["New sympathetic preganglionic neurons arise in the lumbar ventral horn at every spinal level","Vagal parasympathetic preganglionic axons provide postganglionic sweat-gland innervation","Somatic motor axons in lumbar ventral roots become sympathetic postganglionic neurons at the plexus","Sympathetic preganglionic neurons synapse with sensory neurons inside the lumbar dorsal root ganglion","Preganglionic axons descend within the sympathetic chain, synapse, and postganglionic fibers rejoin lumbar spinal nerves via gray rami"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Sympathetic trunks span the vertebral column; descending fibers can synapse at lower levels and return through gray rami to reach peripheral distributions.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Preganglionic sympathetic somata are located in thoracolumbar autonomic regions; fibers can descend within the sympathetic chain to lower ganglia.","B":"The sympathetic supply to sweat glands reaches the spinal nerves via gray rami, not by vagal parasympathetic pathways.","C":"Somatic motor axons do not convert into autonomic postganglionic cells; sympathetic axons travel via established trunk and ramus pathways.","D":"Sensory dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic autonomic synaptic ganglia; the relevant synapses are in the sympathetic chain.","E":"Sympathetic trunks span the vertebral column; descending fibers can synapse at lower levels and return through gray rami to reach peripheral distributions."},"takeaway":"Sympathetic trunks span the vertebral column; descending fibers can synapse at lower levels and return through gray rami to reach peripheral distributions.","recognition":"Gray rami and sympathetic chain descent — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":33,"topic":"L4 · Collagen fiber architecture · Intermediate","stem":"Two collagen-rich tissues differ chiefly in orientation of their dense fibers: tissue A has nearly parallel bundles; tissue B has interlacing bundles in many directions. Which mechanical difference is most likely?","options":["A better resists stress equally in all directions than B","Both must have identical direction-dependent mechanical properties because they contain type I collagen","B better accommodates tensile loading from multiple directions, while A is strongest primarily along the fiber alignment","Matrix tensile resistance depends entirely on proteoglycan ground substance rather than collagen orientation","A is primarily optimized for resisting hydrostatic compression rather than pulling forces"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue distributes tension multidirectionally; dense regular tissue supports longitudinal tension.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Parallel orientation concentrates tensile strength along the fiber axis rather than evenly across directions.","B":"Arrangement matters even when collagen composition is similar.","C":"Dense irregular connective tissue distributes tension multidirectionally; dense regular tissue supports longitudinal tension.","D":"Collagen fibers are major tensile elements; fiber alignment produces strong directional tensile resistance.","E":"Parallel collagen bundles typically support tension rather than hydrostatic compression."},"takeaway":"Dense irregular connective tissue distributes tension multidirectionally; dense regular tissue supports longitudinal tension.","recognition":"Collagen fiber architecture — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":4},{"id":26,"topic":"L3 · Potassium repolarization · Step 1","stem":"A researcher records membrane voltage from two otherwise identical neurons following brief stimulation. In the experimental neuron, pharmacologic inhibition of a delayed outward ionic conductance causes the membrane to remain depolarized longer than in the control neuron. The experimental neuron also fails to develop the usual brief period during which membrane voltage becomes more negative than its resting level. Which altered channel behavior best accounts for both electrophysiologic observations?","options":["Fast inward sodium current","ATP-driven sodium-potassium exchange","Ligand-gated postsynaptic chloride current","Voltage-gated inward calcium current at a smooth-muscle cell","Delayed voltage-gated outward potassium current"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"K+ efflux promotes repolarization and can persist briefly to produce the afterhyperpolarization; suppressing it prolongs depolarization.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The initial sodium inward current produces the upstroke; blocking it would not specifically explain prolonged depolarization with reduced afterhyperpolarization.","B":"Pump electrogenicity contributes to long-term gradient maintenance but does not dominate rapid delayed repolarization.","C":"This is not the characteristic delayed repolarizing current of a standard neuronal action potential.","D":"The experiment involves a neuronal waveform with delayed outward current.","E":"K+ efflux promotes repolarization and can persist briefly to produce the afterhyperpolarization; suppressing it prolongs depolarization."},"takeaway":"K+ efflux promotes repolarization and can persist briefly to produce the afterhyperpolarization; suppressing it prolongs depolarization.","recognition":"Potassium repolarization — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":54,"topic":"L6 · Pelvic splanchnic parasympathetic · Step 1","stem":"Following extensive pelvic surgery, a 58-year-old woman develops urinary dysfunction and impaired autonomic regulation of pelvic viscera. Lower-extremity strength and cutaneous sensation are preserved. Imaging shows no direct injury to the spinal cord, but the surgical field included nerves passing from the sacral region toward pelvic autonomic plexuses. Which portion of the autonomic outflow is most likely interrupted, and how does its normal route differ from the autonomic fibers supplying thoracic and upper abdominal viscera?","options":["Thoracic sympathetic outflow reaching the superior cervical ganglion","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers traveling through white rami at L4","Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2–S4 in pelvic splanchnic nerves","Somatic motor fibers in the femoral nerve","Postganglionic sympathetic fibers ascending from the coccyx to the head"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Sacral parasympathetic outflow uses pelvic splanchnic nerves to reach pelvic plexuses and intrinsic ganglia.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The injury affects sacral pelvic autonomic supply, not head sympathetic innervation.","B":"White rami are associated with the thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow rather than sacral pelvic parasympathetic supply.","C":"Sacral parasympathetic outflow uses pelvic splanchnic nerves to reach pelvic plexuses and intrinsic ganglia.","D":"Femoral motor fibers would affect knee extension and do not primarily provide pelvic visceral autonomic supply.","E":"This route does not account for a sacral pelvic plexus injury."},"takeaway":"Sacral parasympathetic outflow uses pelvic splanchnic nerves to reach pelvic plexuses and intrinsic ganglia.","recognition":"Pelvic splanchnic parasympathetic — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":6},{"id":32,"topic":"L4 · Fibroblast activity · In-House","stem":"A connective-tissue sample shows active spindle-shaped cells with prominent rough endoplasmic reticulum and abundant cytoplasm among matrix fibers. What activity is most characteristic of these cells?","options":["Secretion and assembly of extracellular matrix components","Generation of action potentials in skeletal muscle","Transport of oxygen in circulating blood","Myelination of peripheral sensory axons","Hydrolysis of neurotransmitter in the synaptic cleft"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Active fibroblasts synthesize and secrete collagen and other ECM constituents.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Active fibroblasts synthesize and secrete collagen and other ECM constituents.","B":"Fibroblasts are connective-tissue cells rather than excitable myocytes.","C":"Mature erythrocytes carry oxygen and are not the spindle-shaped matrix-secreting cells described.","D":"Schwann cells myelinate peripheral axons.","E":"Acetylcholinesterase performs that synaptic role."},"takeaway":"Active fibroblasts synthesize and secrete collagen and other ECM constituents.","recognition":"Fibroblast activity — Connective Tissue Histology","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":4},{"id":59,"topic":"L7 · Cervical vertebra identification · In-House","stem":"A vertebra has a relatively small body, a bifid spinous process, and a transverse foramen on each side. To which region of the vertebral column does it most likely belong?","options":["Typical thoracic vertebrae","Typical lumbar vertebrae","Typical cervical vertebrae","Sacral vertebrae","Coccygeal vertebrae"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Typical cervical vertebrae have small bodies, transverse foramina, and often bifid spinous processes.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Thoracic vertebrae have features for articulation with ribs, not characteristic transverse foramina.","B":"Lumbar vertebrae have relatively large bodies and lack transverse foramina.","C":"Typical cervical vertebrae have small bodies, transverse foramina, and often bifid spinous processes.","D":"Sacral vertebrae fuse into the sacrum and do not have the described typical features.","E":"Coccygeal elements are rudimentary and fused, not typical vertebrae with transverse foramina."},"takeaway":"Typical cervical vertebrae have small bodies, transverse foramina, and often bifid spinous processes.","recognition":"Cervical vertebra identification — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":7},{"id":51,"topic":"L6 · Sympathetic white ramus route · Intermediate","stem":"A tracer labels a sympathetic preganglionic neuron in the intermediolateral region of T4 spinal cord. Which route carries its axon into the sympathetic trunk?","options":["Dorsal root → dorsal root ganglion → posterior ramus → gray ramus communicans","Ventral root → mixed spinal nerve → ventral ramus → white ramus communicans","Ventral root → dorsal root ganglion → pelvic splanchnic nerve","Posterior horn → dorsal root → sympathetic chain","Cervical parasympathetic ganglion → spinal accessory nerve"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Thoracolumbar preganglionic fibers exit the cord by ventral roots, briefly join the spinal nerve and ventral ramus, then enter the chain through white rami.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Dorsal roots convey sensory input; gray rami typically return postganglionic axons to spinal nerves.","B":"Thoracolumbar preganglionic fibers exit the cord by ventral roots, briefly join the spinal nerve and ventral ramus, then enter the chain through white rami.","C":"A T4 sympathetic axon neither traverses sensory ganglion somata nor travels through pelvic splanchnic nerves.","D":"The thoracic sympathetic preganglionic cell body is in lateral horn; efferents exit through ventral roots.","E":"This is not the thoracic sympathetic pathway."},"takeaway":"Thoracolumbar preganglionic fibers exit the cord by ventral roots, briefly join the spinal nerve and ventral ramus, then enter the chain through white rami.","recognition":"Sympathetic white ramus route — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":6},{"id":11,"topic":"L2 · Lumbar puncture meningeal layers · Step 1","stem":"A 24-year-old woman is admitted with fever, headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis is required to investigate suspected meningitis. During lumbar puncture, the physician advances the needle through the posterior midline tissues and ligaments, encounters a firm meningeal membrane, and continues toward the compartment containing cerebrospinal fluid. If the needle stops immediately after penetrating the dura mater but before traversing the next meningeal layer, why would free-flowing cerebrospinal fluid not yet be expected?","options":["Pia mater","Posterior longitudinal ligament","Epidural adipose tissue","Arachnoid mater","Denticulate ligament"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The arachnoid lies deep to the dura; traversing it permits entry into the subarachnoid space containing CSF.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The pia closely invests the spinal cord and need not be pierced to sample subarachnoid CSF.","B":"This vertebral ligament is not a meningeal membrane and is not the next layer after the dura.","C":"Epidural fat lies outside the dural sac rather than separating dura from the CSF-filled subarachnoid space.","D":"The arachnoid lies deep to the dura; traversing it permits entry into the subarachnoid space containing CSF.","E":"Denticulate ligaments are pial specializations stabilizing the spinal cord; they do not form the next continuous meningeal boundary."},"takeaway":"The arachnoid lies deep to the dura; traversing it permits entry into the subarachnoid space containing CSF.","recognition":"Lumbar puncture meningeal layers — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":68,"topic":"L7 · Valveless vertebral venous plexus · Step 1","stem":"A 73-year-old man with a history of prostate cancer presents with progressive lumbar pain and several metastatic vertebral lesions. Imaging reveals no continuous tumor extension from the pelvis to the involved vertebrae. The treating physician considers hematogenous dissemination through communicating venous networks surrounding the pelvic organs and vertebral column. Which structural characteristic of these venous connections permits changes in pressure to redirect blood flow and facilitate the transport of malignant cells toward the spine?","options":["Venous spread occurs through perforating branches of segmental spinal arteries","Epidural veins lack connections to paravertebral and pelvic venous networks","Pressure fluctuations cannot alter flow direction in vertebral epidural veins","Competent one-way venous valves prevent reflux through the vertebral canal","Valveless interconnected venous plexuses permit flow direction to change with pressure gradients"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Absence of valves in communicating vertebral venous channels allows pressure-dependent bidirectional blood movement and tumor-cell dissemination.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The described spread uses communicating veins rather than a direct transformation or flow through arteries.","B":"Internal and external vertebral venous plexuses communicate with one another and with regional veins.","C":"Without venous valves, regional pressure gradients can reverse venous flow through connected vertebral plexuses.","D":"The vertebral venous plexuses are characteristically valveless, enabling bidirectional venous flow.","E":"Absence of valves in communicating vertebral venous channels allows pressure-dependent bidirectional blood movement and tumor-cell dissemination."},"takeaway":"Absence of valves in communicating vertebral venous channels allows pressure-dependent bidirectional blood movement and tumor-cell dissemination.","recognition":"Valveless vertebral venous plexus — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":47,"topic":"L5 · Dorsal root ganglion histology · Step 1","stem":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes excision of a mass extending toward the right L4 intervertebral foramen. After surgery, she develops reduced sensation over the medial lower leg and a diminished patellar reflex, although knee extension remains strong. Nerve conduction studies demonstrate abnormalities of sensory nerve responses, while motor-unit recruitment remains preserved. A small biopsy obtained near the operative site shows large neuronal cell bodies surrounded by flattened supporting cells. Which neuronal structure was most likely injured, accounting for the sensory abnormalities without primary destruction of the somatic motor pathway?","options":["Anterior horn of the L4 spinal cord","L4 ventral root","Sympathetic prevertebral ganglion","L4 dorsal root ganglion","Motor end plates of the quadriceps"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The dorsal root ganglion houses primary sensory neuron bodies and satellite support cells; injury can cause segmental sensory loss and reduce the afferent reflex limb with motor output preserved.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Anterior horn damage should primarily produce somatic motor deficit, which is not present.","B":"Ventral roots convey motor efferents; loss would more directly impair motor recruitment.","C":"Prevertebral ganglia contain visceral autonomic neurons and do not convey primary cutaneous sensory fibers of the leg.","D":"The dorsal root ganglion houses primary sensory neuron bodies and satellite support cells; injury can cause segmental sensory loss and reduce the afferent reflex limb with motor output preserved.","E":"A neuromuscular junction lesion would mainly affect contraction, not isolated cutaneous sensation."},"takeaway":"The dorsal root ganglion houses primary sensory neuron bodies and satellite support cells; injury can cause segmental sensory loss and reduce the afferent reflex limb with motor output preserved.","recognition":"Dorsal root ganglion histology — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":7,"topic":"L1 · Synergist/neutralizer—corrected to pronator recruitment · Step 1","stem":"A 28-year-old woman undergoes rehabilitation after a forearm injury. During resisted elbow flexion with the forearm held in a pronated position, she demonstrates intact biceps brachii activation but difficulty maintaining the intended forearm orientation. With increasing resistance, the forearm unintentionally rotates toward supination. The therapist recruits a forearm pronator that counteracts the unwanted rotation while elbow flexion continues. Which functional role does the recruited muscle perform relative to the biceps during this task?","options":["Neutralizer of the undesired rotational component","Prime mover for elbow extension","Antagonist that abolishes all elbow-flexor torque","Passive restraint from the annular ligament around the radial head","Fixator that primarily stabilizes the shoulder girdle"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"A neutralizing synergist counteracts an unwanted secondary action of the prime mover while allowing the intended movement to occur.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A neutralizing synergist counteracts an unwanted secondary action of the prime mover while allowing the intended movement to occur.","B":"Elbow extension opposes the desired flexion and would not selectively neutralize forearm supination.","C":"The desired elbow flexion is maintained, so complete opposition to flexion is not the role.","D":"Ligamentous restraints may influence forearm rotation, but the stem specifies an actively recruited pronator muscle.","E":"A shoulder-girdle fixator stabilizes proximal attachments; the recruited muscle counteracts an unwanted forearm rotational action."},"takeaway":"A neutralizing synergist counteracts an unwanted secondary action of the prime mover while allowing the intended movement to occur.","recognition":"Synergist/neutralizer—corrected to pronator recruitment — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":1},{"id":67,"topic":"L7 · Dorsal scapular nerve localization · Step 1","stem":"A 46-year-old man develops weakness after excision of a mass near the superior medial border of the scapula. Examination reveals difficulty retracting the scapula and elevating its superior angle. Shoulder shrugging remains intact, and pushing against a wall does not produce prominent scapular winging. During surgery, a nerve traveling deep to the levator scapulae and rhomboid muscles was exposed. Which nerve was most likely damaged, producing the combined muscular deficits?","options":["Spinal accessory nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"This nerve innervates the rhomboid muscles and levator scapulae, accounting for combined medial scapular deficits.","optionExplanations":{"A":"This supplies trapezius, and shoulder shrugging would usually be impaired.","B":"This supplies serratus anterior; injury classically impairs scapular protraction and produces medial winging.","C":"This supplies latissimus dorsi, affecting arm extension, adduction, and medial rotation.","D":"This supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus and does not account for the paired rhomboid–levator pattern.","E":"This nerve innervates the rhomboid muscles and levator scapulae, accounting for combined medial scapular deficits."},"takeaway":"This nerve innervates the rhomboid muscles and levator scapulae, accounting for combined medial scapular deficits.","recognition":"Dorsal scapular nerve localization — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":1,"topic":"L1 · Anatomical position · In-House","stem":"A physical therapist documents shoulder abduction, elbow flexion, and forearm rotation during an initial examination. Before comparing the patient's movements with standardized anatomical descriptions, the therapist positions the patient according to the conventional anatomical reference. What is the correct orientation of the upper extremities and palms in this position?","options":["Arms at the sides with palms facing posteriorly","Arms at the sides with palms facing anteriorly","Arms abducted 90° with palms facing upward","Arms crossed over the chest with palms facing medially","Arms at the sides with palms facing the thighs"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"In the conventional anatomical position, the body is erect and the palms face forward, allowing movement terms to be consistent.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Palms facing backward describes a pronated position, not the standard anatomical reference.","B":"In the conventional anatomical position, the body is erect and the palms face forward, allowing movement terms to be consistent.","C":"Abduction changes the reference arrangement of the limbs; this is not the anatomical position.","D":"Crossed arms are not used for the standard anatomical reference.","E":"Palms facing toward the thighs is not the standard palms-anterior orientation."},"takeaway":"In the conventional anatomical position, the body is erect and the palms face forward, allowing movement terms to be consistent.","recognition":"Anatomical position — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":1},{"id":28,"topic":"L3 · Spatial and temporal summation · Step 1","stem":"A researcher records intracellular voltage from a spinal motor neuron receiving excitatory input from two separate presynaptic neurons. Stimulation of either presynaptic neuron alone produces a brief subthreshold depolarization at the postsynaptic cell. When both presynaptic neurons are activated simultaneously, the motor neuron generates an action potential. However, when the same two inputs are delivered several hundred milliseconds apart, no action potential occurs. Which property of graded postsynaptic potentials best explains the difference between the simultaneous and separated stimulation protocols?","options":["Overlapping graded depolarizations sum while temporally separated depolarizations decay before combining","Graded potentials cannot sum because each obeys the all-or-none principle","Temporal summation requires consecutive inputs from different presynaptic terminals","Inputs arriving together cannot summate unless each exceeds threshold independently","Spatial summation requires sequential firing at a single synapse rather than concurrent inputs"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Passive local excitatory potentials can summate at the trigger zone; sufficient spatial and temporal overlap can reach threshold.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Passive local excitatory potentials can summate at the trigger zone; sufficient spatial and temporal overlap can reach threshold.","B":"Postsynaptic graded potentials have variable amplitudes and can add together; all-or-none behavior is a property of propagated action potentials.","C":"Temporal summation can arise from repeated activation of the same synaptic input within a short interval.","D":"Two individually subthreshold inputs can combine to reach threshold when they overlap at the trigger zone.","E":"Sequential activity at one synapse describes temporal rather than spatial summation; spatial summation combines inputs at distinct sites."},"takeaway":"Passive local excitatory potentials can summate at the trigger zone; sufficient spatial and temporal overlap can reach threshold.","recognition":"Spatial and temporal summation — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3},{"id":16,"topic":"L2 · Ventral root motor pathway · Step 1","stem":"A 55-year-old man presents after a spinal injury with loss of voluntary contraction in muscles supplied by one spinal segment. Cutaneous sensation from the corresponding dermatome remains largely intact. Imaging suggests damage to a group of nerve fibers traveling between the anterior spinal cord and the point at which the sensory and motor components merge. Which arrangement of spinal nerve fibers explains the preservation of sensation despite the loss of somatic motor output?","options":["Dorsal root carrying afferent sensory axons","Dorsal root ganglion containing sensory neuron somata","Posterior ramus after spinal nerve division","Ventral root carrying somatic motor axons","Subarachnoid CSF compartment"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The ventral root contains efferent motor fibers; a selective lesion can spare dorsal sensory afferents.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A dorsal-root injury should preferentially impair sensation and reflex afferents.","B":"Loss of ganglionic sensory neurons should produce sensory deficits.","C":"A posterior ramus lesion preferentially affects deep back muscles and posterior skin, not all segmental motor outflow.","D":"The ventral root contains efferent motor fibers; a selective lesion can spare dorsal sensory afferents.","E":"The CSF space itself is not the selective efferent pathway."},"takeaway":"The ventral root contains efferent motor fibers; a selective lesion can spare dorsal sensory afferents.","recognition":"Ventral root motor pathway — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":4,"topic":"L1 · Veins and tissue edema · Step 1","stem":"A 61-year-old man undergoes resection of a tumor in the proximal thigh. The surgical team identifies an artery and two accompanying veins within a common connective-tissue sheath. During dissection, one of the veins is inadvertently compressed by a retractor, while arterial pulsations remain palpable distal to the operative field. Over the next several minutes, the extremity becomes increasingly swollen and mildly cyanotic, although the skin remains warm. Which change in the movement of blood through the regional circulation most directly accounts for the development of these findings?","options":["Impeded venous return with increased upstream capillary hydrostatic pressure","Reduced entry of oxygenated blood into the limb","Increased lymphatic drainage of interstitial proteins","Complete arrest of capillary perfusion","Increased arterial collateral circulation alone"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Venous compression increases pressure proximal to the obstruction, promoting fluid filtration and congestion.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Venous compression increases pressure proximal to the obstruction, promoting fluid filtration and congestion.","B":"Distal arterial pulses and warmth suggest arterial inflow is relatively preserved.","C":"Increased lymphatic clearance would tend to lessen, not cause, swelling.","D":"The findings do not imply complete arrest; arterial inflow continues.","E":"Collateral circulation can preserve flow but does not account for venous congestion following vein compression."},"takeaway":"Venous compression increases pressure proximal to the obstruction, promoting fluid filtration and congestion.","recognition":"Veins and tissue edema — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":1},{"id":45,"topic":"L5 · Anterior horn LMN lesion—corrected segmental localization · Step 1","stem":"A 40-year-old man sustains a penetrating injury affecting the anterior gray matter of the spinal cord at the C6 segment. Several weeks later, he demonstrates persistent flaccid weakness, muscle atrophy, and diminished deep tendon reflexes in muscles supplied by the affected segment. Sensory function remains largely intact, and MRI demonstrates relative preservation of the major descending white-matter pathways. Histologic examination reveals destruction of large multipolar neurons with prominent nucleoli and abundant Nissl substance. Which functional component of the motor pathway has been damaged to produce this pattern of weakness?","options":["Corticospinal fibers traversing the lateral white column","Sensory pseudounipolar neuronal bodies in dorsal root ganglia","Anterior horn lower motor-neuron cell bodies","Postganglionic sympathetic neurons in paravertebral ganglia","Primary afferent processes entering the dorsal horn"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Anterior horn neurons project motor axons to segmentally supplied skeletal muscle; their loss causes flaccid weakness, hyporeflexia, and atrophy at affected levels.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The white tracts are described as spared; descending tract damage would classically cause upper motor neuron findings below the lesion.","B":"Sensory neuron cell bodies lie outside the CNS and do not directly generate the lower motor neuron weakness.","C":"Anterior horn neurons project motor axons to segmentally supplied skeletal muscle; their loss causes flaccid weakness, hyporeflexia, and atrophy at affected levels.","D":"These are autonomic peripheral neurons, not the somatic motor neurons innervating skeletal muscle.","E":"These are sensory inputs, not somatic efferent final common pathway neurons."},"takeaway":"Anterior horn neurons project motor axons to segmentally supplied skeletal muscle; their loss causes flaccid weakness, hyporeflexia, and atrophy at affected levels.","recognition":"Anterior horn LMN lesion—corrected segmental localization — Peripheral Nervous System and Spinal Cord Histology","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":5},{"id":17,"topic":"L2 · Thoracic lateral horn · Step 1","stem":"A 39-year-old man sustains a focal traumatic injury to the upper thoracic spinal cord. Over the following weeks, he develops abnormalities of sympathetic regulation, including impaired sweating and vascular responses below the lesion. Skeletal muscle strength associated with the injured segment remains relatively preserved. MRI reveals damage confined primarily to a portion of spinal gray matter lateral to the central canal, without extensive disruption of the major ascending and descending white-matter tracts. Which population of autonomic neurons would be most directly affected by this injury?","options":["Preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the intermediolateral cell column","Somatic sensory neuron cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia","Lower motor neurons of the anterior horn","Postganglionic sympathetic neurons of the paravertebral chain","Primary parasympathetic neurons of sacral segments"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Thoracic lateral horn gray matter contains sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies for autonomic output.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Thoracic lateral horn gray matter contains sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies for autonomic output.","B":"Sensory cell bodies are in peripheral ganglia outside the spinal cord.","C":"The anterior horn largely supplies skeletal muscle motor activity and is described as preserved.","D":"Those cell bodies lie outside the spinal cord.","E":"Sacral parasympathetic cell bodies are not in the injured thoracic region."},"takeaway":"Thoracic lateral horn gray matter contains sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies for autonomic output.","recognition":"Thoracic lateral horn — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":50,"topic":"L6 · Two-neuron autonomic pathway · In-House","stem":"A student compares somatic motor output to skeletal muscle with autonomic motor output to cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands. What is the usual arrangement of an autonomic efferent pathway?","options":["A single direct motor neuron with soma in a peripheral ganglion","A direct skeletal-muscle motor axon followed by a sensory neuron","Three sequential lower motor neurons entirely within the CNS","A CNS preganglionic neuron synapsing with a peripheral postganglionic neuron that reaches the effector","A sensory ganglion followed by a single somatic motor neuron"],"answer":"D","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The standard ANS motor pathway has a two-neuron chain from CNS to smooth or cardiac muscle and glands.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Typical autonomic efferents use two neurons, with the first in the CNS.","B":"This is not the autonomic motor chain.","C":"The normal autonomic efferent chain includes a peripheral autonomic ganglion.","D":"The standard ANS motor pathway has a two-neuron chain from CNS to smooth or cardiac muscle and glands.","E":"Primary sensory ganglia are part of afferent rather than efferent organization."},"takeaway":"The standard ANS motor pathway has a two-neuron chain from CNS to smooth or cardiac muscle and glands.","recognition":"Two-neuron autonomic pathway — Peripheral Nervous System: Somatic and Autonomic","difficulty":"In-House","lecture":6},{"id":60,"topic":"L7 · Anterior longitudinal ligament hyperextension · Intermediate","stem":"After a rear-end collision, MRI shows strain of a ligament running along the anterior surfaces of the cervical vertebral bodies. Which movement should place the greatest additional tension on this ligament?","options":["Moderate cervical flexion only","Axial rotation of the forearm","Lateral flexion of the lumbar spine","Scapular elevation against resistance","Excessive cervical extension"],"answer":"E","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The anterior longitudinal ligament resists vertebral hyperextension and can be strained in whiplash.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Flexion is more directly opposed by posterior ligamentous structures.","B":"Forearm rotation does not specifically stress an anterior cervical ligament.","C":"The injured structure is in the cervical region.","D":"Scapular elevation primarily involves muscles rather than anterior vertebral ligament tension.","E":"The anterior longitudinal ligament resists vertebral hyperextension and can be strained in whiplash."},"takeaway":"The anterior longitudinal ligament resists vertebral hyperextension and can be strained in whiplash.","recognition":"Anterior longitudinal ligament hyperextension — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":7},{"id":15,"topic":"L2 · Patellar reflex afferent limb · Step 1","stem":"A 42-year-old patient undergoes evaluation after an injury involving the upper lumbar region. The patellar tendon reflex is markedly reduced on one side. Voluntary knee extension remains strong, and electrical stimulation of the motor nerve produces a normal quadriceps response. However, rapid stretching of the quadriceps does not produce the expected reflex contraction. Which component of the pathway between muscle stretch and anterior horn motor-neuron activation is most likely impaired?","options":["Quadriceps motor end plate","Primary proprioceptive afferent traveling through the dorsal root","Quadriceps contractile filaments","Anterior horn cell’s efferent axon","Posterior ramus cutaneous afferent fibers"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Interruption of sensory input from muscle spindles disrupts the reflex even when the efferent neuron and muscle can function.","optionExplanations":{"A":"A normal response to motor nerve stimulation makes a major neuromuscular junction failure less likely.","B":"Interruption of sensory input from muscle spindles disrupts the reflex even when the efferent neuron and muscle can function.","C":"Normal electrically elicited contraction supports intact muscle contractility.","D":"Strong voluntary extension and an intact electrically evoked motor response argue against a major efferent lesion.","E":"Cutaneous afferents from the posterior ramus are not the primary proprioceptive limb of the patellar tendon reflex."},"takeaway":"Interruption of sensory input from muscle spindles disrupts the reflex even when the efferent neuron and muscle can function.","recognition":"Patellar reflex afferent limb — Overview of Nervous System and Spinal Cord","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":2},{"id":2,"topic":"L1 · Muscle contractions · Intermediate","stem":"During rehabilitation after a knee injury, a patient slowly lowers the body from standing into a chair. Electromyography confirms continued quadriceps activation throughout knee flexion. When the patient subsequently rises from the chair, the same muscle group remains active while the knees extend. How do the mechanical characteristics of the quadriceps contractions differ between these two movements?","options":["Concentric during both movements","Isometric during lowering and eccentric during rising","Eccentric during lowering and concentric during rising","Eccentric during both movements","Concentric during lowering and isometric during rising"],"answer":"C","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Active quadriceps oppose knee flexion while lengthening during lowering and shorten to extend the knee while standing.","optionExplanations":{"A":"During controlled lowering the quadriceps lengthen while active; this is not concentric shortening.","B":"The knee moves in both phases; rising requires shortening of the actively contracting quadriceps.","C":"Active quadriceps oppose knee flexion while lengthening during lowering and shorten to extend the knee while standing.","D":"The quadriceps shorten as they actively straighten the knee during rising.","E":"The quadriceps do not shorten during controlled lowering and the knee angle changes during rising."},"takeaway":"Active quadriceps oppose knee flexion while lengthening during lowering and shorten to extend the knee while standing.","recognition":"Muscle contractions — Introduction to the Musculoskeletal System","difficulty":"Intermediate","lecture":1},{"id":70,"topic":"L7 · Transverse atlantal ligament · Step 1","stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents after a motor vehicle collision with upper cervical pain and restricted head rotation. CT demonstrates a small fracture involving the dens of C2, with preservation of the occipital condyles and the superior articular surfaces of C1. The physician explains that the affected bony projection normally serves as the central pivot for rotation of the atlas. Which ligament is principally responsible for retaining this projection against the anterior arch of C1 and maintaining stability during rotation?","options":["Transverse ligament of the atlas","Anterior longitudinal ligament","Ligamentum flavum","Nuchal ligament","Posterior longitudinal ligament"],"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"The strong transverse component of the cruciform ligament passes behind the dens and restrains it against the anterior arch of C1.","optionExplanations":{"A":"The strong transverse component of the cruciform ligament passes behind the dens and restrains it against the anterior arch of C1.","B":"It runs anterior to vertebral bodies and primarily limits hyperextension.","C":"It connects laminae and does not clamp the dens against C1.","D":"This posterior cervical ligament primarily limits forward flexion and serves as a muscle attachment.","E":"It runs posterior to vertebral bodies and helps support discs, not directly retain the dens against the atlas anterior arch."},"takeaway":"The strong transverse component of the cruciform ligament passes behind the dens and restrains it against the anterior arch of C1.","recognition":"Transverse atlantal ligament — Vertebral Column and Back","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":7},{"id":31,"topic":"L3 · Na K pump gradient maintenance · Step 1","stem":"An isolated skeletal muscle preparation is exposed to an inhibitor of ATP-dependent transmembrane ion transport. Over several minutes, normal resting electrical activity is initially preserved, but prolonged exposure causes progressive loss of the intracellular and extracellular sodium and potassium gradients. The muscle eventually loses its capacity to sustain repeated electrical responses. Which relationship between active ion transport and passive channel-mediated ion movement explains why the electrical dysfunction develops progressively rather than immediately?","options":["Ion gradients exist only when acetylcholine is present","Preexisting electrochemical gradients initially sustain passive channel currents, but without ATP-dependent pumping they cannot be maintained over time","The pump generates each millisecond-scale action potential directly","Potassium ions become electrically neutral during prolonged inhibition","Inhibition of active transport immediately closes every voltage-gated channel"],"answer":"B","hint":"Identify the key clinical or mechanistic clue, then compare the answer choices.","explanation":"Channels carry ions down existing gradients; repeated flux slowly dissipates gradients when the pump is inhibited.","optionExplanations":{"A":"Established ionic gradients are present at rest, independent of momentary neurotransmitter release.","B":"Channels carry ions down existing gradients; repeated flux slowly dissipates gradients when the pump is inhibited.","C":"Fast voltage-gated channels generate the action potential; the pump maintains the gradients enabling repeated firing.","D":"Potassium remains charged and the issue is loss of electrochemical gradients.","E":"Pump inhibition need not instantly disable existing voltage-gated channels."},"takeaway":"Channels carry ions down existing gradients; repeated flux slowly dissipates gradients when the pump is inhibited.","recognition":"Na K pump gradient maintenance — Ion Channels and Action Potentials","difficulty":"Step 1","lecture":3}]}}};